      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Will the Riding Skirt Disappear from the Hunting Field? MATCHLESS ENGLISH HOME.</head>
        <p>Will the Riding Skirt Disappear from the Hunting Field? MATCHLESS ENGLISH HOME.</p>
        <p>Knole House, Lord Sackville's seat, near Sevenoaks, is described as the most EngFsh of all English homes, but its history is linked w ith that of several monarchs. Henry VIII., who was a good judge of a residence, took it from the Archbishops of Canterbury, although Cranmer euphemistically resigned it to him. In Edward Vl.'s time the Earl of Northumberland obtained a grant of it, but on his downfall Queen Mary restored it to the Archbishops, and Cardinal Pole resided there. Elizabeth, however, bestowed the house and estate upon her favourite, Lord Leicester, North- umberland's son. Within a few years he returned it to the Queen, who then granted it to Sir Thomas Sackville, for a reason which throws a sidelight on the social condition of England at that time. The Queen wished to keep Sir Thomas near her Court, and being aware that the roads to Buckhurst—his seat in Sussex were impassable during nine months of the year,she wished him to reside at Knole, which was much nearer London. HOUSE ALMOST LIKE AN ANCIENT TOWN. This Sir Thomas Sackville was created Earl of Dorset by James 1., and he, with Archbishop Bourchier, a great grandson of Edward 111., may be termed the makers of Knole. The Archbishop pur- chased the estate from Lord Say and Sale, and built on it the original house, to which wing after wing has been added. In its architecture, paintings, panellings, carvings, furniture and treasures, this wonderful country seat, which contains 365 rooms, is unique. It is a house of gorgeous galleries, the finest being the Cartoon, the Leicester and the Brown. James Us bedroom, with its wonderful bed and beautiful old hangirg4, with the Venetian, Reynold's and Spangle bedrooms, and Lady Betty's dressing-rooms, are the most noted. Nor is the interior only wonderful ; the exterior is equally mar vellous. Everywhere are ancient archways leading into courtyards, each bearing its distinctive name, or into fair gardens and ancient pleasaunces. From the rising ground around a splendid view is obtained of the whole structure, whose long line of quaintly gabled and turreted rocifs is broken by the principal tower-capped gateway. Up to its grey walls and long windows, grows the green turf, whilst the beautifully wooded park—seven miles in circumference —with its glades and greenswards, its slopes and valleys, on which the brown deer graze, presents a magnificent panorama that 1-ses itself in the weald beyond. Knole is, in fact, as matchless for its setting and surroundings, as it is for the magnificence of its interior and the beauty of its architecture. RIDING ASTRIDE.</p>
        <p>It seems highly probable to judge from results at Olyrop'a that the picturesque riding skirt will soon be as obsolete as the crinoline, since all the young feminine competitors rode astride at the International Horse Show, notwithstanding the fact that the King does not approve of this custom. When the skirt is worn, it shows the tops of the boots quite as much as the goat, for it is in reality an apron only. Those women who have once ridden astride never wish to return to the side saddle, but in order to acquire the grip with the knees it is necessary to begin quite young. Possibly the skirt will disappear from the hunting field when these young riders grow up, for the side saddle is less safe as well as less comfortable, and makes mounting and dismounting much less easy if accom- plishment. E VOGUE FOR DRAPERY. As usual, the freakish models that are so startling when they make their appearance at the beginning of each season, have now settled down into reasonable, and in some cases, graceful modes. Every day adds to the vogue for draped effects, which are de rigitear for evening wear. The fashionable afternoon frock is draped also ; indeed, draperies have invaded almost every garment of women's cutdcor attire. Nothing could be m )re graceful an I beautiful than many of the draperies worn at this season's special functions, but these were the work of the artist in dress, and the skilled manipulation of beautiful materials ; in other cases the eficct was disastrous. Draperies offer opportunities for the blending or con- trasting of colours, denied the straight plain skirt, and special advantage was taken of these outlets for effects in the dresses prepared fer the races. An underskirt affords a basis for cue culcur, whilst the draperies may be of another. Pale pink, pale straw, and mauve pink, have all been mest successfully used under the fltmiest draperies, in black lace, tulle, cr chiffon, for many of the handsomest gowns.</p>
        <p>SIMPLE DRESSES FOR OUTDOOR FUNCTIONS.</p>
        <p>Quite a contrast to these elaborate toilettes are the simple frocks prepared for the young girl for Henley garden pat ties, and other outdoor functions. Not only are the materials of which they are made most fascinating, but each is given a distinct individuality by means of the deftest touches of embroidery, or other trimming, or even by buttons. A new specimen of the button maker's art is of pearl, shaped like 'a currant, which is easily removed when the dress is sect to the laundry. Many girls this season are very wisely laying in a stock of these pretty dresses rather than investing their allowances in one or two elaborate toilettes. Already there are signs that the rage for Cubist designs or materials is subsiding, and the beauty of many of the Pompadour patterns in tiny flowers is now appreciated for those charming and girlish frocks. With lingerie dresses of all descrip- tions, the collar gives a novel cr rather distinctive note. The small Medici in wired lace or tulle is very popular, but it must be very well cut to be effective. HATS AND MUFFS.</p>
        <p>Hats for wearing with these dresses are tco numerous and various to enumerate. The soft silk hat, or very fine chip, veiled and trimmed with tulle, and many coloured flowers locks well with the Pompadour cottons or foulards. The broad-brimmed sailor with its ruching oi silk tulle or white moire is another novelty. Ruches are much used on skirts and corsages in Paris, where a new make of tulle has made its appearance. It has a charming Pompadour design on a pale background, and this, with crepon and mousseline, is used for the most charming dresses for young girls, the skirts of which are very finely pleated frcm waist to hem. The summer muff, that had its origin in America, is among the fashionable accessories this season. In tulle, for evening or theatre wear, it is both charming and suitable, but as an accompaniment to an out-door toilette, is merely a burden, when a bag or parasol has also to be carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>COMMAND PERFORMANCE AT KNOWSLEY.</head>
        <p>COMMAND PERFORMANCE AT KNOWSLEY.</p>
        <p>After the dinrer at Knowsley on Monday evening a variety programme of great excellence was placed before the guests. It is interesting to note that the treatment cf the commodious conservatory, trans- forming it into a charming bijou theatre, was carried out by the well-known film of Messrs. Waring and Gillow, Ltd., Bold-street, Liverpool. The colour scheme was cream and sapphire blue. There was special feature in the background of the fernery, where a fountain was arranged with large blocks of ice, and with electric fans conveniently placed. Every care was taken to keep the atmosphere delightfully cool, special attention being given to the ventilating of the theatre. The seating accommodation for the Royal party was luxuriously furnished with gilt furniture in rich brocade of the Louis XV. periel. The remainder of the auditorium was furnished with handsome gilt chairs and cushions covered in rose-du-barrio silk. In front of the stage the orchestra were screened by banks-of plants sod beautiful flowers, &amp;c., and group- ings of floral decorations from the Knowsley conserva- tories were placed in every conceivable position,which formed a charmirg and pleasing tout ensemble. The whole of this elaborate scheme has been specially designed and Carried cut by Mr. R. 8. Johnson, ably assisted by the staffs of various departments of the well-known firm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>NEW BOOKS.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS.</p>
        <p>Economic Liberalism. By Herman Levy, Ph.D. London : Macmillan and Co., Ltd. 4s. Gd. net. This is the English edition of a book published in Germany in 1902, but it has been brought up to date and refers to such recent legielation as the Budget of '909 and the National Insurance Act. At such a time .ee the present, when Liberalism seems tO have taken a new lease of life, it is particularly opportune that a book dealing with its rise and growth should appear. For it is a study of the origins of the Liberalism that we have known in the past, and which seems so widely different from the Liberalism of the present day. Dr. Levy sees the beginnings of Liberalism not in the dectt ines of the Manchester school, but in the struggle of the peop!e in the 17th century for religious and constitutional liberty. And he shows very clearly how this great struggle involved eccnornic questions of immense importance. "In view of the extraordinary importance," says Dr. Levy, "of religicus emancipa- tion to the general life of each individual in the 17th century, one can hardly avoid the impression that, in addition to the direct influence of religion on economics, their indirect interaction, though difficult to prove, must have been considerable. For the majority of the lalecuting and middle class, the achievement of religious freedom was the necessary preliminary to all other occupations, especially to industrial activity, which was for the believer, particu- larly for the Puritan, only conceivable on a religicus foundation. Until this foundation was srcure from external attack religious impulses were continually diverting men from industry, either by obvious and material means such as emigration, money prices, or commercial ruin, or because religious problems distracted and absorbed men's inner powers and pre- vented them from putting forth their full working capacity. Throughout the pericd religicus perse.ci..i- tion and religious dissension overcloud economic views of life." Dr. Levy traces the change that came gradually over the ccuntly, very largely through Puritan influences and gave birth to that freedom which we possess to-day, and he supports his account with a wea!th of illustration drawn from various sources, but princi- pally from the large collection of pamphlets in the Biitish Museum, dealing not only with those strictly economic questions, but with constitutional, ecclesias tical and general political movements, which only indirectly affected industrial problems. We think that Dr. Levy exaggerates the opposition between the new spirit which he discerns in the political world of to-day and the princieles of economic Liberalism. We believe that it is possible to trace the continuity of th new with the old. But we certainly agree with Dr. Levy in his concluding paragraph. "The general effect of all these various phenomena," he says, speaking of the conflicting tendencies of the present time, "is to deepen the cenvicti na that iii England to-day the old more than ever clashes with the new. Any year may bring the decisive paint In whichever direction, however, the stream finally sets, and whatever may be the changes in the relation of class to class and in the social and economic con- dition of England, the significance of the economic Liberalism of the past for certain features of English industrial life remains. Economic Liberalism taught England to believe in the rights and greatest possible development of the individual; to regard each man as equal before the law, and to display toleration towards the opinions of others whether in politics or in religion: to place the s me social value on all professions, and to respect what other nations and races hold holy. To other nations these and other characteristics of Liberal culture are still novel and unfamiliar. The English- man wiil not lcse them even in der a new social system, for they have bec me an integral part of his national character." Dr. Levy has produced an excellent book which is really indispensable to the political student. The get-up is in Messrs. Macmillan's best style,</p>
        <p>"When Lionheart was King." By Wallace Gaindy. London : George G. flarrop. Ccloured frontispiece and eight other illustra- tions by Jack Orr. Cloth extra. Gilt top 3s. Gd:w. Mr. Gandy is incliced to think cur romancers over- look the good old English story,and the volume before us, "When Lionheart was King," is the outcome cf his allegiance to the rative spirit. The book, which is charmingly illustrated, deals with peasant life in the North of England, duting Lionheart's gorgeous exploits in the Floly Laud. George-a-Green, its hero, is a character we are bound to love. He is the orphan son of a free-man, and as we follow George in his rural tasks, the pageant of mediaeval domestic life passes before us. We join the boisterous revel at an old-time wedding, when weddings were solemnised outside the churchdoor. We hear the breezy talk of housewives las they chaff the cheap-jacks at the fairs. The lighter side of the roguery of the period is vividly set before us as the steward of the manor dis- penses justice at the Court of Pie Powder. A mystery play, the words, of course, arranged for pr( sent-day readers, throws much light upon those early days, and in his arrangement Mr. Gandy reflects the old spirit, and mirrors the outlook upon life of characters. That was a famcus fight at Wakefield when George-a-Green won the pindership, and with the same cudgel George continues to perform loyal service for the absentee king. Robin Hood and his merry men try their strength with the redoubtable pinder, but the pinder is the best man, and a firm friendship springs up between Ahem, as also between their respective maidens, Maria, and the beautiful Beatrice Grymes. At last Lionheart comes home, and hearing of George-a Green in London, determines to see this paragon of prowess with Irsi own eyes. It happens that the King and his escort-come upon George and Robin when the latter are spoiling to fight anybcdy. Consequently, the incognito king gets a few blows to his credit. But Richard loved a man ' who was a man, and this encounter led to a pretty scene in which there was some talk cf marrying. We think Mr. Gandy has caught the spirit of the old times. This straightforward tale of a simple-hearted folk should appeal to all boys, young and o.d. The antiquary would find it interesting, fcr, granted a necessary amount of literary licence, the story abounds in the filk lore, and the social history of the times. For the sake of readers who enjoy a pleasant spin along the paths of literature, the author provich s a preface which deals with these stories of old English life and manners. The volume is handsomely got up. The publishers are well-known for their admirable work in this direction, and " When Lionheart was King" maintains their reputation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>PIMPLES ON FACE AND NECK.</head>
        <p>PIMPLES ON FACE AND NECK.</p>
        <p>3, Hoppit-lane, Droylsien, nr. Manchester, Eng.- - " I suffered for several years wi:h pimples on my face and neck. 1 used to be ashamed to go out. My face was one mass of pimples; you couldn't have -put a pin where there wasn't a pimple. They used to matter and run and I could not sleep with them. They used to fairly torture me. "I took a treatment for three months, which was a waste of money, so I decided to try Cuticura Soap and Ointment. The first _week I cculd see a great change, so I continued it for three months. I only had three boxes of Cuticura Ointment and used the Cuticura Soap to wash with. They acted like magic and they gave my skin a nice fresh healthy look and cured my pimples." (Signed) Findlay Hene:y Taylor, January 31st, 1913. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post-free from nearest depot. Address: Newbery, 27,Charterhouse Sq., London; R. Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N. S. W.; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay; or "Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Men who shave and shampoo with Cuticura Soap will find it best for skin and scalp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Bible Class. Mr. J. J. Firth's Farewell.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Bible Class. Mr. J. J. Firth's Farewell.</p>
        <p>Sunday was the occasion of the farewell visit of Mr' J. J. Firth, the Police Court Missionary, before leaving the town to take up his new appointment at St. Helens. Speaking of the story of Gideon, Judges 6 and 7, he applied it to the present-day Church. Just ES Gideon was a man of the people, so the leaders of the present time often come from the masse3. Further, the quali- ties required in Gid4on's 300 were required in the ranks of modern Church life. Willicgness, courege, and eager common sense were the outstanding qualities required by God in Gideon's chcsan band, and these qualities were essential to success in modern religious work. Those who were not possessed of these, those who creaked end prophesied" It can't be done" were better outside. They might applaud, but the work was left to the willing few. The thanks of the class for Mr. Firth's past cervices, and their wishes for his future welfare and success were voiced by the President (Mr. J. C. Holland), and Mcscrs.. Herbert Tonge and James Hamblett.</p>
        <p>immimmum</p>
      </div>
