      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>ROYALTY AND LOYALTY.</head>
        <p>ROYALTY AND LOYALTY.</p>
        <p>Stirring Scenes in the Streets</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>of Warrington.</head>
        <p>of Warrington.</p>
        <p>A MEMORABLE VISIT.</p>
        <p>f—Warrington's proud history is the richer lir. the events of Monday when their reca)psties King George V. and Queen Mary arse,iv.ed a fervently loyal welcome on their ho " Visit to the old town. It was no small Btan°llr for the borough to be chosen as the th,rting Point for their Majesties' tour ),ugh the County Palatine, and the inhabi- rs were fully alive to the distinction. The eePtion accorded to the Royal visitors had a aolciinely whole-hearted Lancashire flavour, tb Its warmth and enthusiasm did justice to br feeling of the whole county. Looking (jack on the memorable happenings of a great bay, there is no room for any other spirit te. that, of complete satisfaction. Their 4)esties were much touched by the e'va,sgaific'ent greeting which awaited them at De'rY point of the route, while the towns- sb.°Ple were delighted at the special favour fl to-Warrington by their Majesties in 05 CA _ stre iuusly consenting to ride .through the aticiets in an open carriage. Everybody, young a,o, had a good view of their Majesties, rot:i this was regarded as ample compensation sao the disappointment experienced on the riPion of the flying visit of King Edward in 1909. it.uphe town looked bright and picturesque in aft iestal trappings. Best of all, the weather, ant a morning of hesitancy between rain 10,. fair, added that finishing touch without asuleb the decorator's art would have been ariPc'ught. Shortly before their Majesties' th,.17a1, the clouds dispersed, the sun broke co„tugh and, with very slight intermission, tCned to smile on the town throughout !in' Majesties' stay of one hour. tb-1. salute of twenty-one guns heralded kir Majesties' advent to the whole town to neighbourhood and placed the waiting Tilliltitudes on tenterhooks of expectation. fate° sumptuously-appointed Royal train arned into Arpley Station, which was sTllsitely decorated, a few minutes before! eZueduled time—three o'clock. The informal gat'acter of the visit was emphasised by the attire of the King, bW,ll° Wore a brown lounge suit, with a grey overcoat, and a brown ler hat. In his buttonhole was the floral emblem of the county „e red rose. The Queen was very charmingly dressed in a pale teen costume, with la biege straw hat profusely titimmed with jdrangeas. Only a short time was given to the f.Omaliti( s of a4ctrodu6tion, and their Majesties emerged from the station to the en!,°,2nPaniment of a roar of cheering from the people gathered in the eo'„'re• The three State carriages provided by Lord Derby for the ieveYance of their Majesties and suite had a quaint old-world Ret.liresqueness which imparted exactly the sentiment which a 041,1 progress needs. They were grand old barouches hung on Coal'uer springs, horsed with fours, each horse dark brown in le,°,lllr with black points, the. Wheelers seventeen bands and the po—ers about sixteen-two. On the backs of the horses sat he 4 sbilions in the Derby livery—buckskin breeches; blue coats )7,4 braided in white, and little black jockey caps on curled 41.te periwigs. ,t a walking pace the carriages traversed Bridge-street and 4"Y-street, all densely thronged with cheerirg crowds, and thi,eeled into the east drive of the Town Hall grounds. In front of oriLeivic mansion the most imposing ceremonial of the day was L of Their Majesties left their carriage and ascended the steps 1311,‘,1° Royal dais—a stately structure gorgeously draped in (Wile and gold—where they were welcomed by the Mayor ncillor Dr. Joseph). Then came that distinctly human note resich for the time being dispels formality and strikes a ehil3PlBive chord in the hearts of, all. Ten thousand of the school t4.en were massed on the lawn and, in astonishingly perfect 4,"te. and time for such a vast choir, they sang the first verse of the ebTaonal Anthem. Then they waved red, white and Vile handker- burs and cheered. The unique picture of colour, animation and eajtant enthusiasm thus produced greatly pleased the-Queen, who Dised the. King's special dttention to it. Afterwards the Mayor ea.ented a number of prominent townspeople who had the honour Toakiilg hands' with their Majesties. The crowning feature of the, 41;4 Ha:11 ceremonies was .performed just before their Majesties daio'rture. The King, by touching an electric button on the Royal (Veiled the first seetion of the new Warrington Bridge, 11 he had seen and admired on his way to the hall. •A g/A:ttrY gun boomed out to announce the accomplishment of the Tac 1Y hopeh • n, amid tumultuous cheering, their Majesties left the Town and drove to the works of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield andlSons, Oo ?Led, to gain their first impressions of Lancashire industrial life. trip Ileir ‘way they passed a pathetic group, consisting of the Posit.'" of the town, who had considerately been allotted special 148 I°°B to view their Majesties in comfort. The inspection of hoo."8 osfield's works occupied less than three-quarters of an chatted buk their Majesties were delighted with all they saw, and 01des, with several workpeople, notably George D.lGleave, the, co/111e" !,3tnPloye, whose simple narrative of his sixty-three years and ac"oli with the firm was listened to with sympathetic interest on ?Predation by the King. 11,1otopeaving Messrs. Crosfield's works, the Royal party entered 41e cars in which they rode to Knowsley Hall, where they are passiguests of Lord Derby. Whilst in the borough, and again in eteeenj through Penketh and Widnes, the cars slowed downto an BPleticeglY moderate speed, enabling all the onlookers to gain a Ae 411 'view of the King and Queen. aotb visit of their Majesties was purely unofficial the military thrr, ies were not held responsible for their safe progress "ugh the town. The duty of preserving the line of route con- ego_ the Tntlday Y fell upon the police,and the local force was augmented for b Th Y a large number of officers from neighbouring towns. Ettrao—n.'nef Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls) had made perfect off e,_6°lllents, and the smOothness with which everything passed ther,,'";ned the cordial commendation of the King. Prior to the visit tv aad been persistent runiotirs as to suffragist intentions, but e,Was not one single untoward incident to mar their Majesties' t 0 triePhtoawl nPr. ogress, and the day's proceedings did distinct credit</p>
        <p>THE DEeORPITIONS. TT-, eir Majesties See Something of "Beautiful 4, To Warrington. tale 4.ee our great industrial county in its everyday aspect was ZaisatilliarY object of the Royal visit to Lancashire, and their laad intimated that they idid not wish municipalities aco'agreat expense on lavish decorations. It was probably on b„,-unt that the official scheme of embellishment at Warrington 1;111°?a.-1:abvs,9, elaborate as on the occasion of the visit of King vv`ghtti,, -IL Nevertheless, sufficient was done to impart a festal 4tl lea and picturesqueness to the streets, and the general effect llot to Pleasing. The Reception Committee wisely decided 171,4 the architectural beauty of the Town Hall beneath a e 9' b bunting and festoons, and the decoration of oeivie r mansion was confined to the placing of el lovely flowers on each window sill, In</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>LONG LIVE THE KING :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>Photograph by the Topical Press Agency. BRIDGE STREET : THEIR MAJESTIES AT MARKET GATE.</head>
        <p>Photograph by the Topical Press Agency. BRIDGE STREET : THEIR MAJESTIES AT MARKET GATE.</p>
        <p>front of the hall the Royal dais—the scene of the ceremonies of the day—had been erected, This structure was most handsomely canopied and draped with purple velvet and gold braid, while the Royal coat of arms and several erowns,all worked in gold,heightened the regal dignity of the pavilion. Around the dais were white handrails with yellow hangings. On leaving Arpley Station square, their Majesties passed under a handsome archway with Corinthian columns and a white trellis banner with the inscription "Welcome to</p>
        <p>Lancashire," Bridge-street readily lends itself to decorations, and the tradesmen had risen to the occasion, with the result that there was a fine array of shields, garlands and loyal mottoes. Over Market Gate appeared a large shield, bearing the arms of the borough, from which festoons of floral garlands were carried to the adjoining buildings. A column was erected at each of the four corners, and overhead appeared the greeting "Welcome to Warrington." Sankey-street was spanned with lines of streamers, and flags fluttered from every</p>
        <p>building. The Education Office and the Health Department were effectively treated with natural flowers. At the entrances of the east and west drives of the Town Hall were handsome trellis and floral arches, bearing the Royal monogram and portraits of the King and Queen. The electric standards along the whole of the Royal route were artistically decorated with garlands, baskets of .hanging flowers, shields and trophies. At the Sankey Bridges boundary of the borough was an imposing arch, the motto taking the appropriate form of "Good-bye."</p>
