      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>ARRIVAL Al ARPLEY,</head>
        <p>ARRIVAL Al ARPLEY,</p>
        <p>Their Majesties' Impressive Welcome to Warrington.</p>
        <p>UNCEREMONIAL RECEPTION.</p>
        <p>Photograph by Lafayette, Dublin. HER MAJESTY THE QUEEN.</p>
        <p>Impressive manifestations of loyalty were witnessed at Arpley Station where their Majesties first set foot in the County Palatine. From an eatly hour the people began to throng the barricaded streets, and by two o'clock every available point of vantage round Arpley square was ccupied by those who were eager to catch the first glimpse of the royal party. Terri- torials of the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regi- ment, under the command of Lieutenant-Colonel B. Faitelough, and members of the West I ancashire A.S.C., under the command of Captain Picton, looked exceedingly smart as they were formed up round the pquare, aid many were the expressions of admit ation. ['be 'drum Etna fife band played lively airs and popular selections, and the music, coupled with the evident enthusiasm of the people, relieved the long wait of any tediousness. A mild sensation was caused at about 2-45 when the pilot engine steamed into the station, which had undetgoue a great and pleasing transformation at the hands of the *zit corators. The platform at which their Mojetities were to alight had been covered with crimson cloth, with a margin of white, and the ordinarily drab walls were hidden by light hangings panelled in gold and green, relieved with shields and overhung witji flags. Awaiting the arrival of the Royal train on the pl‘tform were the Earl and Countess of Derby and their daughter, Lady Victoria Stanley, Lord Shuttlewotth (Lord-Lieutenant) and the Hon. Miss Shuttleworth, Mr. J. W. Makant (High Sheriff) and Mrs. Makant, Mr. H. P. Lane (Chief Con- stable of the county), Mr. W. M. Turnbull (district railway superintendant at Liverpool), and Mr. A. Fletcher (one of the directors). The time for the art ival was fastdrawing near, and the excitement of the crowd increased with each passing second. The station officials reported "A minute before time at Crewe" on the Royal special, and as the first cf the 21 guns, which roared out the Royal salute, sent forth its glad!: message, necks were craned to catch a glimpse of the train whlch steamed from the direction of Bank Quay, just before three o'clock. The train, which is a very magnificent one,consists of seven saloon coaches, and was drawn by the powerful engines " Loyalty " and "St George," two of the latest triumphs of Crewe works. The town clock was chiming the hour as his Majesty stepped from the coach, appearing a trifle fatigued after his long journey. In a moment, how- ever, his face was wreathed in smiles as Lord Derby • stepped forward to receive him. Following a moment later, the Queen was welcomed 'there Lord Derby and the Countess of 'Derby. Besides the members of the suite there travelled in the Royal train Sir Gilbert Claugbton (chairman of the London and North-Westernll Railway Company), Sir Frank flee (general manager), Sir Robert Turnbull (superintendent of the line), and Mr. W. H. Williams (head (,1 the carriage department), who was in charge of the j urney. The receribn of tfieir Majesties was distinctly uncere- monial. As soon as the folding steps of the railway carriage were lowered, the King eescended to the platform and slioOk hands warmly with Lord Derby, Lord Shuttleworth, and afterwards wit} other prominent representatives of the county, who were introduced to him by Lord Derby. Those presented included Ali-s Shuttlewo-th, Mrs. Makant; Sir Robert Turnbull and Mr. H. P. Lane (Chief Constable of the county).</p>
        <p>The Royal pirty were conducted tin ough .the passage which ordinarily leads to 4 the district manager's office, instead of through the booking hall. The appearo ance of their Majesties was the signal for an outburst of cheering, which was ad pronounced that it almost drowned the strains cf the National Anthem, played by the Territorial band. All the pant-up feelings of the people were freed and the cheering was renewed as the King and Queen entered the first of the three open carriages provided by the E;rrj of Derby. The second carriage contained the Countess of Derby, Lady Ampthill, lord Annaly and Lord Stamfordham ; whilst in the third carriage were 1,4c1y Victoria Stanley, Sir L. Fitzvvilliam, Lord Charles Fi•zmaurice, and Major Clive Wigram. About half-an-hour before tire King arrived there was a litt'e excitement at Arpley Station. As the privileged spect dors were conversing in small groups there was a sudden alarm of fire, the decorations on the m tin wall having ben ignited, it is suppsed, by a carelessly thrown cigarette or lighted match. The flames were ere ping upward before they were discovered, but were beaten out before any appreciable damage was done. All traces of the _affair were hidden with a few yards of fresh m aerial before the arrival of the Royal train. Owing to the early discovery of the outbreak, the whole incident occupied only a few seconds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>KING'S MESSAGE TO LANCASHIRE.</head>
        <p>THE KING'S MESSAGE TO LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Famous Industries Which Depend upon Workers' Devotion and Energy.</p>
        <p>On reaching Knowsley iq the late afternoon their Majesties were presented with an address from the County Council, whose chairman (Hr. W. Scott Barrett) was knighted by the King. in the evening the King and Queen and Lord Derby's other guests attended a variety performance in the private theatre at Knowsley. After receiving the address of the Lancashire County Council, the King said :</p>
        <p>On behalf of the Queen and myself I thank you very heartily for your kind address of welcome to my Duchy and County Palatine of Lancaster. Your county, so often the scene of momentous events in our history, has now become a hive of Industry, and is connected by innumerable ties with all parts of the Empire- The Queen and I retain vary pleasant and grateful memories of all that we saw and learned during our visit to you last year, and we hope on this occasion to renew and extend still further our acquaintance with the Industries for which your county is famous, and with the men and women upon whose devotion and energies, under Providence, those industries depend. I trust every worker in your populous county willTfeel that the continued development of the industries of Lanca- shire is work of national and imper a importance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ALONG THE ROUTE.</head>
        <p>ALONG THE ROUTE.</p>
        <p>The procession presented a stately and picturesque sight as it traversed the main streets to the Town Hall. Their Majesties were delighted with the splendid enthusiasm displayed by the huge crowds and repeatedly bowed and smiled in acknowledgment. There was an exceptionally striking incident in Bridge-street. Three trumpeters were statioued on the roof of the premises of Messrs. W. Hodgkinson Ltd., and on the approach of their Majesties greeted them with a fanfare, afterwards playing the National Anthem. People who had stoodbehind barriers for several hours felt amply repaid by the excellent view which they had of the King and Queen, and on all hands we heard expressions of great satisfaction that their Majesties had conferred the special favour upon Warringtoa of riding through the streets in an op2n carriage.</p>
        <p>(Continued on Page 4.)</p>
      </div>
