      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>AT THE TOWN HALL.</head>
        <p>AT THE TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>Huge Loyal Crowd awaits the Arrival of the King and Queen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SLOW DRIVE PLEASES THE PEOPLE.</head>
        <p>SLOW DRIVE PLEASES THE PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>A GREAT WAVE OF ENTHUSIASM WELCOMES THEIR MAJESTIES TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Two hours before the time fixed for the arrival of the Royal party the stat.ds in fiont of the Town Hall, where the chief event of the day was to take place, begin to wear an animated appemance. lintnediatt ly in front of the Town Hall ste!s was a dais sell elevated (4 the forethought of those who had had the making of the plans with Lord Derby as guide),Eo that all in the vicinity would have a chance of %itnessii g the rare and inter( sting ceremony that was to be enacted. On each side of the dais were st ,nds care- fully erected tnd numbered, which were I laced in charge of Town Hall officials, who saw them occupied promptly and without the slightest sign of confusion. On the lawn in front were 'Laser(' the Fchool children of the borough. That short period of waiting was ohe of hcpe and fear as to the weather, but the sun triumphed, and by thiee o'clock when the Looming of the guns that fired the toyal salute which announced the snivel of the royal train at Arpley station, the clouds had dispersed, a typical July day was assured, and all settled down with the assurance that the j y of the occasion was to be unalloyed by any rain. It was soon apparent that his Majesty was showing every consideration to the masses of people who were lining the streets on his way to the Town Hall, fur though the distance is shott the time that passed between the signal of his an ival and his apprLsch to the Town Hall was long—and it, of course, seemed longer to the anxious and expectant throng. Five minutes passed, and these grew into ten minutes before their M .jesties came near enough to allow of the cheeis that rollt d in waves along the line of route t reach the ears of those at the Town Hall. When, at last, those cheeis did come sefficiently close nearly a quarter of an hour had elapsed, but all impatience was di counted by the remembrance that his Maktsty's horses must have come through the town at a slow walk, a thoughtful act that formed the subject of the most favourable comment.</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN AND THE eHILDREN.</p>
        <p>By this time those who were to be favoured by being presented to their Majesties had ascended the loyal dais to be ready for the arrival. As the Royal procession came slowly by the east gate leading through the gardens to the Town Hall, and as it drew up in front of ti at historic building amid the singing of the National Anthem, unfurling of the Royal standard, and the unrestrained cheeting from young and old, the scene was highly impressive, and the King and Queen appeared to be delighted with the surroundings into which they had suddenly been plunged. The salting thousands were quite as delighted ith the fine spectacle with which they were being favoui ed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>THEIR AULIESTIES' INTEREST IN THE SeENE.</head>
        <p>THEIR AULIESTIES' INTEREST IN THE SeENE.</p>
        <p>THE QUEEN AND THE</p>
        <p>The King wore a brown kuuge suit, a dark grey overcoat w ith brown velvet collar, ar.d a brown bowh r hat. His Majesty was wearing a red rote iu honour of the county he was himself honouring by a visit.</p>
        <p>_ Her Majesty was attired in a cloth costume in her favourite colour, eau de nil,cut in simple line at.d style, trimmed on the corsage and down the shirt with fine braidings to match. The large beige stiaw bat was flat brimmed, trimmed with a profusion of hydrangeas. Her Majesty's umbrella matched her gt,wn in colour. It was interesting to note the effect made upon the King and Queen resFectively by the ovation of which they were literally the centre. His Majesty was so busily engaged recognising the v. ciferous cheering from the stands that be seemed to be oblivit us of the fact that on his left ten thousand young voices were cheering for all their tender throats were woith, and that they were waving their tri-co!ou.red fllgs with an</p>
        <p>enthusiasm that knew no bounds. But be;: Majesty's ear, true to her motherly iusiinct, beard the slmut and Eaw ti e demonstration, and hefote the dais was reached, she had touched the King on the arm, and drawn his attention to what was really the mc at charm- ing scEne of the afterm on. His MajPsty Emiled kindly at tLe children, and raised his hat to give them a friendly wave— a reward which was duly appreciated and recognised as only children could recognise such atteutioia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>PRESENTATIONS</head>
        <p>THE PRESENTATIONS</p>
        <p>The presentations to their Majesties were got through very smartly in full sight of the people. First Lord Dei by presented The MAYOR (Councillor Dr. Joseph) and The MAYORESS (Mrs. Arnold Nield). The Mayor then presented the following ladies and gentlemen in the order shown : The DEPUTY MAYOR (Mr. Alderman Forshaw) _ _ _</p>
        <p>, FREEMAN OF THE BOROUGH (Mr. Robert Pierpoint, J.P.). MRS. PIERPOINT. SIR GILBERT GREENALL, BART., C.V.O. LADY GREENALL. MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P. MR. A. H. CROSFIELD. MRS. A. H. CROSFIELD. MR. JOHN FAIRCLOUGH, J.P. MRS. JOHN FAIRCLOUGH. MR. COUNCILLOR ROBERTS, J.P. MRS. ROBERTS. MR. ALDERMAN BOLTON, J.P. MRS. BOLTON. Mr. ALDERMAN SMETISURST, J.P. MRS. SMETHURST. MR. ALDERMEN PIERPOINT, J.P. MRS. PIERPOINT. MR. ALDERMAN BENNETT, J.P. MRS. BENNETT. MR. COUNCILLOR WILKINSON, J.P. MRS. JOHN WILKINSON. ADDITIONAL PRESENTATIONS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>"What a Pretty Sight '</head>
        <p>"What a Pretty Sight '</p>
        <p>Her Majesty, upon the present ation of the Mayoress, referring to the children, said</p>
        <p>What a pretty sight: How many children are</p>
        <p>massed on the green ?</p>
        <p>The Queen also asked as to the size of the</p>
        <p>town ard the number of factories there</p>
        <p>were in the central portions of it</p>
        <p>Her Majesty also conveyed the impression that she would have been delighted could</p>
        <p>arrangements have been made to allow of</p>
        <p>a visit to the bridge to open it on the spot. She then shook hands with the Mayoress and said "Good-bye."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>MR. FIND MRS. ARTHUR CROSFIELD INVITED TO KNOWS LEY.</head>
        <p>MR. FIND MRS. ARTHUR CROSFIELD INVITED TO KNOWS LEY.</p>
        <p>Before their Majesties left the Town Hall Lord and Lady Derby invited Mr. and Mrs. A. H. Crosfield to Knowsley to attend the entertainment they were giving the same evening in honour of the King and Queen. This marked courtesy was accepted, and Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield had the pleasure of being present at the intercsting and historical Knowsley event.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>ING PRESENTED BY LORD DERBY.</head>
        <p>ING PRESENTED BY LORD DERBY.</p>
        <p>on the cotsage. Over it she was wearing a coat of bright blue ribbed silk, faced with white satin and a black hat with drooping paradise plume. Mrs. Arthur Crusfield was a particularly picturesque figure among those selected to p irtake of the royal favours of the day. Exceedingly becoming and according admirably with her graceful bearing was a gown of deep maize colour with its shaped tunic of the same shade and its edging of honey-coloured lace. '1 he touchrs of burnt orange were most artistic, a remark whi :h applied with as great a force to her brown hat with shaded paradise plumes.</p>
        <p>Mrs. John Fairclough had a gown of black brocade with white panel on the skirt, veiled with black lace and a black and white bonnet.</p>
        <p>M s Henry Roberts's gown was of grey brocade drai.ed and relieved li3htly with lilac, while her toque was trimmed with pink shaded lilac blossoms. Mrs. Robert Bolton wore a handsome gown of grey watered silk.</p>
        <p>Mrs. W. Pierpoint was attired in black and white</p>
        <p>Mrs. Arthur Bennett's gown was of cornfhwer hue brocade with draped skirt. Mrs Wilkinsin woro a costume of grey watered silk and a black hat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>Photograph by Birtles, Warrington.</p>
        <p>WAITING THE ARRIVAL OF THE KING, TO BE PRESENTED. From left to right: Alderman W. Pierpoint, Mr. Robert Pierpoint, Mrs. Robert Pierpoint, Alderman Forshaw, Councillor Henry Roberts,Mrs. Henry Roberts, Mrs. John Fairclough, Mrs. W. Bolton, Mr. John Fairclough, Alderman Smethurst.</p>
        <p>Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., Lady Greenall and Sir Gilbert Greenall behind the Mayor and Mayoress.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, the Mayoress, Alderman Bolton, Mrs. Smethurst, Mrs. A. H. Crosfield, Councillor John Wilkinson, .Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Mrs. John Wilkinson, Mrs. Pilkington, Mr. Lionel Pilkington, Mrs. Arthur Bennett, Alderman Arthur Bennett, Mr. J. J. Webster, Mr. A. Thorne, Mr. C. Cole (Re wton). The shield in front of the dais is one of the "King George " trophies presented to the elementary schools for competition in athletics. Insets :—(1) The King's arrival : Stepping from the royal carriage. (2) The departure: Her Majesty descending to the carriage.</p>
