      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>KING OPENS THE NEW BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE KING OPENS THE NEW BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The presentations over, his Majesty, by pressing an electric button fixed on the Royal dais severed a rope stretched across the north end of the completed section of the new Warrington bridge, and the firing of a gun in the neighbourhood of the river announced that the plan had been successfully carried out. • Loud cheers followed this landmark in the history of Warrington; and the loudest cheer of all avas given as the Royal procession resumed its journey, by way of the west gate; to the works of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. At this point a• procession of 'citiz3n s headed by the Mayor, walked to Om new bridge by way of Sankey-street and Bridge: street, to complete the formality of opening. Many people still lined those thorout hfares, and showed a lively interest in the Fie was much gratified by the reception i proceedings. It was pleasant to notice the m which had been accorded himself and the mixed nature of the procession, exemplified it Queen. Everything had been very ..}IJ by the one outstanding fact that Sir Gilbert nice indeed. H had had, he said, a good view of [ Greenall, Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., and Mr.</p>
        <p>MR. LIONEL E. PILKINGTON, J.P. (Chair- man of the Newton-in-Makerfield U.D.C.). MRS. PILKINGTON. MR. CORNELIUS COLE (Clerk to the New- ton-in-Makerfield U.D.C.)</p>
        <p>M. JOHN J. WEBSTER, M. Inst., 0.13., of Westminster, the Engineer, of the Bridge; and</p>
        <p>MR. ALFRED THORNE, of the firm of Messrs. A. Thorne'&amp; Sons, of Westminster, the. con- tractors.</p>
        <p>the bridge, which was a fine,structure.</p>
        <p>Crosfield were companions on the journey. - -</p>
        <p>His Majesty also asked questiOne 'regarding</p>
        <p>They received the plaudits of the populace in</p>
        <p>the weather, and seamed pleased when</p>
        <p>a collective form -And seemed to- enjoy the</p>
        <p>informed of the manner in which the occasion had been favoured.</p>
        <p>experience. Mr. Robeet Pierpoint walked 'dqwn to the bridge rather late and was largely: recognised and cheered,an experience ;-witich was • repeated in a more marked degree when he walked back in the proces- sion to the Town Hall. In the Town Hall grounds refreshments were served, and a delightful impromptu garden party, upon which the sun shone in a way that was all the more welcome because it was so unexpected, brought an official end- ing to what had been for many thousands of people one of the most interesting and enjoy- able times of their life. - -</p>
        <p>THE KING FIND OUR VERY</p>
        <p>FINE BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>When the King shook hands with Mr.' J. J. Webster, the architect of the bridge, his Majesty said that</p>
        <p>The request msde to him to open the bride and the opportunity he had had of doing so had given him much pleasure. It was, ,he said, a very fine bridge.</p>
      </div>
