      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>MAYOR BE</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR BE</p>
        <p>THE DRESSES OF THE LADIES.</p>
        <p>The Mayoress looked especially charming in a graceful gown of pale blue veiled and draped and having a deep collar of honey-coloured lace.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Robert Pierpoint wore a costume of navy blue satin cbarmeuse and a dark hat</p>
        <p>Lady Greenall wore a dark blue silk costume cf pretty design, made with a scalloped tunic flout,ced ith blue net and having wide, white tulle fichu frills</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>INVITATIONS. OCCUPANTS OF STANDS.</head>
        <p>THE INVITATIONS. OCCUPANTS OF STANDS.</p>
        <p>No authentic list of those who were present at the ceremony in front of the To.°/0,11, Hall can be given, as many collective ilivitr tions were sent out. There were, howeNt.% some 600 persons on the stands, includillir members of the Town Council and the,, friends, Corporation officials, ex- MaY°rees Borough Magistrates. Local County gagia. trates, Education Committee, represent.e, tives of Board of Guardians, Rural Distri--! Council, Warrington Infirmary, Nursing Association, Red Cross Public Officials, Bank Managers, ClergYo'l,, of all denominations, Officers of Territoria' Army Service Corps, and public men. THE RECEPTION COMMITTEE r, was composed of the following :—The gOet, the Deputy Mayor, Aldermen Smethurs Pierpoint, Burton, Tinnion, John James Evans, Bennett, Bolton; Councinun Birtles, Hawthorn, Skelland, Wilkinso"o: Lashford, Craik, Chantler, Pember,t% R3berts, Locker; the Director of Educitt°t": the Chief Constable, Major Green,Lieutenan Colonel Fairclough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>WHO'S WHO?</head>
        <p>WHO'S WHO?</p>
        <p>siog The Mayor (Csuncillor. Dr. Joseph) is eeellP' 9- that honourable position for the third year in 50C for atoll. He came to Warrington in 1874, and Weet .0 some years house surg ton at the Infirmary, at,tirB„siss the old institution in Buttermirket-street. De 'the the third nie heal officer of health f rWarring'l9l' first medical superintendent of the Fever Hospital,es9, the first public analyst. His &amp;tighter, the SlB3"„reor was married to Mr. Aroold Nield on 30th, 1912. al) The Deputy-Mayor (Alderman W. J. Flshst3to was Mayor of Warrington from the years I°l'A-his 1911.. He is a native of Warrington and receive" J 6 early education at the National Schools, tess afterviarsit becoming a foundation scholar at the 13100 School. He was articled to Mr. Brook, a former ti clerk of Warrington, and became a solicitor is Ito the first scholar of the Blue Coat Sebe°,:gity, qualify for the profession. During Ids Maye— is the Alderman took a great interest vo furthering the Warrington Bridge proj=ct, sod Air a strong advocate of an 80 feet bridge, which (2.°e:or, ally received the approval of the Council. As m:1-00 he had the honour of receiving King Edward VID' his visit to the town in 1909. isetad Mr. Robert Pierp riot, the most recently e'ssfor honorary freeman of the Borough, was M•P• •sir Warrington from 1892 to 1906. He is a stsarrl-_„ t Dian and his emineut services to his native Ob.; were fittingly rec splised by the Council in 1911, vi 0, they added his name to the illustrious roll of freemeb, Sir Gilbert Greesall,B irt., C.V.O.,is known tbre,ll,gsss out the country for his keen interest in all 1313ry concerning the agricultural industry. He is holier:be director of the R Tit Agricultural Society, andhits Wart ington Agricultural society is indebted t°,eoeir for many acts of generosity during his long preal" 3f that organisation. He succeeded to the•title os leath of his father ii July, 1891 His marriage Miss Griffith took place on October 25 h, 190).