      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 12, 1913,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>DISTRICT NEWS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS.</p>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>DEATH oe Mn. WILLIAM DRIVER.—We regret to announce the death of Mr. William Driver, of the Grove, Lymm, who passed away after a long illness on Wednesday morning. Mr. Driver was for many years head gardener at Lymm Hall. CONGREGATIONAL CHRISTIAN SERVICE BAND.—A well attended meeting was held in the school vestry on Monday evening, when Mr. Harry Perrin gave an address from the words found in Proverbs " He that winneth souls is wise." Mrs. P. Leather presided. ALLEGED ATTEMPTED SUICIDE —OR Sunday, P.C. Gould arrested John Ashton, ironworker, of 47, Lime- street, Miles Platting, Manchester, on a charge of attempting suicide, on Sunday afternoon, by cutting his throat with a razor, near Big Oak Tree Farm, High Legh. Ashton received medics] treatment from Dr. Thorpe, and on Monday afternoon appeared befoie Major J. E. G. Groves and Mr S. Davies, and was committed for trial at the next Knutsford Quarter Sessions. FLOW P.R SERVICE.—A flower service was held at the Bros m Edge Primitive Methodist Chapel last Sunday afternoon, conducted by Mr. 11. Champ, who gave an interesting address on "Seeds and Flowers," illustrated by numerous specimens. Recitations were given by Mrs. Lawson, Annie Blease, Jennie Reid, Elsie Scott, and Joe Blease ; and the beautiful flowers that were brought by the scholars were sent to the Altrincham Hospital. INDUCTION SERVICES.--Newly-appointed ministers ccromesced their duties at two of the local Noncon- formist Churches on Sunday. Prior to the Rev. R. W. Ferguson taking charge of the morning service at the Primitive Methodist Church, the hymn " We bid thee welcome" was sung. At the Baptist Church the Rev. E. Perker conducted the first part of the morning service, af Let which the Rev. E. E. Lovell preached an inspiring sermon. ( ENDEAVOUR SOCIETY.—The snnirersary of the Christian Endeavour Society connected with the Zion Indep.cdent MethoclA Chapel was cslebrated on Sunday, when Mr. Robert Bold, of C;oft, was the preacher at the morning and evening services. He also addrrsed the scholsrs in the afternoon, and in the evening preached a helpful sermon from the text " Ye must be born again." 111is.s Blanche Rathbone sang a solo at the latter service, Miss Greenman being the organist. An early morning prayer meeting formed part of the anniversary. WESLEYAN GARDEN PARTY.—By the kind permission of the Rev. J. H. Pa elyn, tba local Wes!e, ems held their third annual garden party in the picturesque grounds of " The Hamlet," Reddish, Lyrnm, on Wednesday. Despite the unfavourable weather conditions, there was a very fair attendance, including the Revs. J.ll Pewlyn, Morgan, and 11. Midolewo: d. During the afternoon teas were served and tarticipated in with a relish given by the fresh air. The programme included songs by Madame Wilsc..n, whose interpretations of " April Borg, " " The strong box " " Toe sea hath its pearls," " Somewhere a voice is calling," and "Little grey home in the \Vest " were highly appreciated ; solos by Miss Winstanley •, duets by the Misses Rogerson end Win- s'anley ; and recitations by Miss Moston and Mr. W.ll. Finch. A hat-trimming competition, won by Mr. Margott ; ball-catching contest, won by Miss Gladys Dale : a shooting rang:, and a cscoanut shy els-) aided to the attractions. There was also a stall of fancy and plain goods under the charge cf Mrs. H. S. Brown and Miss Holford ; and a sweet stall attended to by Mrs. A. H. Hudson and Miss Furness. The proceeds were in aid of the up-keep of a Mission Child, and local funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>SALT WORKS FATALITY. New Regulation Suggested at the</head>
        <p>THE SALT WORKS FATALITY. New Regulation Suggested at the</p>
        <p>Inquest.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, held an inquest at Warrington oe Saturday, concerning the death of Samuel James Shaw (30), 46, Winwick-road, Warrine- ton, and Thornae Nixon Wright (21), Woodbine-terrace, Lymm, who sustained terrible scalds by the bursting ot a steam pipe at the salt works of Messrs. Chart 22 Moore and Ce., Heatley. Mr. Ire'itad, U.L. Inspector, watched the proceedings for the Home Office. Mr. Turner, of Manchester, represented the firm, and M. E. A. Connor, of Warringtore appeared for the relatives. The circumstances of the tragedy were rel ited by William Lyon, boiler foreman. He stated that at 10.30 on the previous Wednesday he was firing up two boilers when he suddenly heard a noise like the loud puff of an engine. On looking round he saw a cloud of steam surrounding ano ',ter boiler. He at once ~ent for a key to turn off the hot water supply and raised an alarm. Owing to the terrific heat he was at first unable to turn off the steam, but he eventually did so by means of a hook. The deceased men were engaged in cleaning out one of the flues and when assisted out they were shockingly scalded. From the evidence of Mr. Wm. M. Shaw, engineer at the salt works, it appeared that when the b Ater was blowing off steam a pipe which had become badly corroded, burst and the flue in which the deceased were working was flooded with steam. The pipe was after- wards examined, and it was found that there was a crack about 2,1„ inches wide and 12 inches long. There was no f periodical inspection of the pipes to see whether they I were corroded or not, hut the firm made a calculation as to how long the pipel ought to live, and at the end of that period they were renewed. If it had not been for the frazture in the pipe, the deceased men could not have possibly been endangered in cleaning out the flues. It was not usual to mike th 3 men leave the flues when stesm was being blown off. The witness L:oe, in answer to the Coroner, said that before this accident he would not have suspected any danger in the men cleaning out the flue while steam was being blown off. The Inspector, addressing the jury, said the engineer was justified, to a consieereble extent, in thi !king there was no particular need fer en examination of the pipes, because they were open to the atmosphere at one end. The corrosion, however, miler, have been ,ery severe,and indicated the importance of fairly frequent examiva- tion. He did not think there was any particular danger in allowing the men to work in the flue while steem was being blow off. It was quite usual. The boilers were under the exp.rt examination of the inspectors of the insurance cmtpany and they did not examine the pipes—indicating that they did not consider there was any apptecieble danger attaching to them. Mr. Turner said he was instructed by Dr. Trantom,one of t'le directors of the firm, to state that ie future it would be made a regulation that during the blowing off of steam no man should be allowed in the flues.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a verdict of ---'-ic.Cldental death," and approved the suggested new regulation. • Mr. furnr raid this was the first time, during the ten years that the works had beea open, that a serious accident had occurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RECOGNITION OF -THE REV. E. E.</head>
        <p>RECOGNITION OF -THE REV. E. E.</p>
        <p>LOVELL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Parker's words on the qualities of those associated with the church, and congratulated Mr. Lovell on this brilliant prospect. He conveyed the good wishes of the Brighton Grove students for a successful ministry. Mr. Greenwood raid that the bond of brotherhood was the one that filled the churches, and he begged that Mr. Lovell might to helped with a prayerful sympathy. Mr. A. Price said that Mr. Lovell was one of the best of ministers,and be had come to see if the Lymm people were wot thy of hi 3 old pastor. He spoke of Mr. Lovell us a young people's man, a regular visitor, one to whom they could look up to and confide in, and said that he had left a deep impression on the Christian life of Whitchurch. Mr. A. Whittingham spoke of his fourteen years intimate friendship with the Rev. E. I. Lovell and his family at Whitchurch and eulogised his work. Mr. Wynne-Jones spoke of Christian character, and gave a personal testimony of Mr. Lovell's worth. He urged the necessity of loyalty to the pastor, and unity of aim and purpose. Mr. J. D. B. Smith, as the hon. secretary of the Free Church Council, extended a hearty welcorre to Mr. Lovell. He said that many churches made the mistake of expecting too much from the minister, forgetting that" the rank and file "must do their best. The Rev. E. F. Lovell, speaking of his coming to Lymm, slid that he was a lover of the country and thought that le had come among a people he would learn to love. He spoke brie fly on "The melting of Christian character" and " Conversion," stating his belief in the convers'on that came about from a gradual growth. He proposed a comprehensive vote of thanks to the caterers, chairman, speakers. and soloises. This was seconded by Mr. J. J. Ilurstfield, and carried unanimously. The proceedings were enriched with the solos " Friend ' and "Jerusalem "sung by Miss M.Brundrett and Mr. J. 11. Brazendale respectively.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening the Higher-lane Baptist Church celebrated the acceptance of their pastorate by the Rev. E. E. Lovell with a public tea, after which a r cogni- tion servic , was held in the schoolroom. Mr. Edward Clarke, of C tdishe,d, presided over a good attendance. and was supported by the Rev. E. E. Lovell, Rev. Parker (Leicester), Messrs. Greenwood, and Sc. agg, late s'udent pastor-in-charge ; A. Whittingham, Whit- church ; A Price and E. IVynne-Jones, Warrington ; J. D. 13. Smith, 3. H. Holt, and G. L. Welford. Mr. E. Clarke, in speaking of the great difficulty of keeping a church going without a resident pastor, said that they had made a wise choice in Mr. Lovell, and he appe ded to the church members to strengthen the bands of their minister. Piactical Christianity was the need of the age, and such, he believed, Mr. Lovell would a._,sist them to attain. On behalf of the trustoes he conveyed their good wishes and promise of !:.sist- ance. After the Rev. E. Parker's long expelience of Lyrnm he cerild say, whilst introducing the audience to Mr. Lovell, that he could no:, dud a more kind, faithful and loyal people than at Lymm. He said that the way they had carried on the work of the church since the Ray. A. J. Back's removal WO bees wonderful, and in a few earnest words he told his audience how they might help Mr. Lovell :—(1) Think the world of your pastor, and do not be ashamed to let those around you know how well you like him ; (2) help him through your daily lives by being earnest, sincere, and consecrated witnesses of Christianity ; (3) prayer. best minister in the world cort!cl not carry on the church in the right way unless the members were supporting him by prayer. Mr. Scragg, in the course of a breezy speech, endorsed</p>
