      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>WHAT WARRINGTON WANTS TO</head>
        <p>WHAT WARRINGTON WANTS TO</p>
        <p>KNOW.</p>
        <p>From week to week, from year to year, there have been appearing in these columns, statements made by local people, which we have all read with great interest, and many of us with great profit. But what we want to know is, do they stand the greatest test of all —the test of time? Here is conc'usive evidence on this point from an Earleatown man : Yean'y six years agl, Mr. D. Boyes, ii 20, Sheffield- row, Vulcan, Earlestown, said : My work is a strain on the kidneys, and I suffered for some time from stiffness and pain in the hack. I often loan I it difficult to get about after I had been stooping. My back was SO bad lne time that I was away from work for three months with it. I notic.sd, top, that when my back was bad, tie excre'ions fr..m the kidney s were ut natural and c_ntti.ied gravel. " But 1 am glad t ) say I found a re'isble remedy in Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. They soon cleared the excretions, and eased the backache. Now I always keep a b x by me and I can well recommend them. (Signed) 1). Boyee." NEARI Y SIX YEARS LATER, Mr. Boyes said : " My back and kidne3s have not bothered rue much amen Doan's Backache Kidney Pills did me so much good. I take a few doses of the pills when I feel a touch of backache, and they soon set me right again." Price 2s. 9il. a box, 6 boxes -13. 9d. ; of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co ,8, Wells-street, Oxfsrd- street, London, W. Don't ask for backache and kidney pills—ask distinctly for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. Boyes bad.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WALTON.</head>
        <p>WALTON.</p>
        <p>GARDEN THIEF FINED.—At the Stockton Hetith Petty Sessions on Tuesday, John Gable, 6, Atherton- street, Bank Quay, Warrington, was charged with steal- ing vegetables value 4i., and roses value 3d., from gardens at Walton Superior, between July sth atAl 6th. He admitted the offences.—Henry Bell, Hatton-lane, Walton Superior, who owned the vegetables, said be was growing them for "show " purposes. He was aroused shortly after four o'clock in the morning of July 6th by the police and found the vegetables missing.—John Edward Jackson, also of Hatton-li.ns, spoke to being aroused by the police and finding some of his roses had been pulled.—lnspector Breese s Aid he was cycling in plain clothes in the district at 3.30 s.m. on July 6th, and came across the defendant carrying the vegetables. He told witness he had bought them, but he (witness) pointed out that they had been " pulled " end not cut. Defendant then admitted having taken them, and showed witness the gardens. The Inspector further stated that a consider- able amount of damage had been done lately in this way.---A fine of LI was imposed.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.' GARDEN SOCIETY.—On Thursday evening, a goodly number of the members of the above society evaded themselves of the privilege, most courteously accorded by Mr. 11. Houghton, of the Dingle, Appleton, in throw- ing open his grounds and gardens for inspection. The unique collection of cacti, as well as the orchids, were mu3ll admired, and the splendid borders of heibace )us plaits, roses, &amp;c., gave much pleasure to the visitors. HILL CLIFFS BAPTIST SCHOOL TREAT.—Upwards of 250 scholars and adults availed themselves of this very pleasant afternoon outing to Whitley on Friday of last week. Tea was provided at Mr. Ban Bradford's farm, kindly lent for the occasion. Numerous games, races, &amp;c., were indulged in by the young people. A novel feature of this outing was that it sas led by the newly-formed Boys' Brigade under the command of Capt. Tom Moss. This was augmented by the Bugle Band from tha Latchford Baptists. Iltarty votes of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WALKING DAY TRAGEDY.</head>
        <p>WALKING DAY TRAGEDY.</p>
        <p>A Boy Who Gave His Life to Save,</p>
        <p>His Friend.</p>
        <p>VICAR'S TRIBUTE.</p>
        <p>"He lost his life in trying to save his friend," was the comment of Coroner Brighouse on Saturday, when he held the inquest on the body of Harry Johnson (14) son of James William Johnson, saddler, 16, Edgeworth- street, whose tragic end cast a gloom over the Walking Day treat in connection with St. Paul's Sunday School. Thomas Welsby (14), 31, Mill-lane stated that at 12.40 on Friday afternoon, he was walking along the canal bank at Bess say, in company with his step-brother, Redvers Overeud, Harold Leigh, and the deceased. When near the swing bridge at Bewsey Lodge, witness picked up a stone, intending to throw it into the water. His foot slipped and he fell into the canal. He sank once and on coming to the surface, made an unsuccess- ful attempt to clutch the bank. He struggled and deceased then held out his hanl and gripped witness by the fingers. As he did so, his foot slipped and he, too, was immersed. He then caught witness by the shoulders and pushed him under. Witness tried to get hold of the bank again, and deceased relaxed his hold of him. Witness then succeeded in getting hold of the side, while deceased struggled and ssent further out into the canal. Ilarold Leigh then grasped witness and he managed to regain the bank. The Coronsr : It was a miracle this lad was not drowned too. _ _</p>
        <p>Welsby, continuing, s tid he shouted and a man Came along the bank. He stopped at the bridge and then rate to Bewsey Lodge. Mr. Eastwood came on the scene with a prop, but decea,ed had sunk. Walter Leigh (12 69, Wakefield-street. said he went to Bi-wsey-Pdrk with the °I ject of attending St. Paul's Walking Day trf at, but the gate was not open. He saw deceased ad his companions coming along the bank and was a wi nns of the fataltty. Witness eventu— ally grasped Welsby's hand and pulled him to the side. Mr. Eastwood gave evidence as to the recovery of</p>
        <p>_ the body and the unsuccessful Ennis at artificial respiration. lt gas stated that the boys had climbed over the</p>
        <p>canal bridge to get in the p;sition where the accident occurfet _ _</p>
        <p>A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned. The Rev. W. Bracecamp informed the Coroner that deceased was formerly a member of St. Paul's Sunday School,and some of his companions were scholars there. The tragedy cast a gloom over the whole of the Walk ing Day festivities, and he (Mr. Bracecamp) was asked by the teachers to attend the inquest to express their sympathy with the parents in their terrible bereave- ment. Deceased was an excellent lad, for whom he (Mr. Bracecamp) had great admiration. Mr. Bracecamp also paid a tribute to Mr. Eastwood and Acting- Sergeant Newey for their hard work in endeavouring to restore animation.</p>
      </div>
