      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>" "I I</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="4" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Ski</head>
        <p>Ski</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday July 12, 1913.</p>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>A FEAST OF ROYALTY AND LOYALTY.</p>
        <p>Now that the greater part of a week has passed since King George and his gracious Consort began their triumphal progress through Lancashire, at Warrington, we can look leisurely back upon the great occasion and judge it in its various phases and in fitting perspective. A feeling of delight- ful satisfaction at everything connected with the visit has . been prevalent, a satisfaction that grows deeper as time pro- oeeds. To begin with, the weather was perfection. Never did village maiden, who was queen-elect, view with graver con- cern the promise of the skies on a first of May morning, than that with which the whole of the populace regarded the dawn of Monday. There had been heavy rain in the night, and the outlook was drab in the extreme; but every minute the Royal train was rushing north added brightness to the day, and by the time the Royal visitors reached the Town Hall, the scene was bathed in a flood of sun- shine that gave it the one finishing touch that every heart desired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>NOTHING BUT PLEASANT MEMORIES.</head>
        <p>NOTHING BUT PLEASANT MEMORIES.</p>
        <p>There can be -little doubt that their Majesties were highly gratified by the welcome they received on touching Lanca- shire soil ; there can be as little doubt that 'the people of this loyal and ancient borough were as delighted with the Royal favour that had been bestowed upon them. Thera are no memories but pleasant ones in the wake of the visit. On the last occasion of a Royal passage through the town there were some heartsearchings,but subsequent events proved that there were reasons why King Edward so hurriedly left Warrington behind him. In twelve months the King was dead, the saddest of all confirmation of the explanation made at the time of the need there had been fora speedy journey to Knowsley. On the present occasion no such miscarriage has occurred, and to Lord Derby,who had charge of the arrangements, the best thanks of the town are due. Under his Lordship's guidance the Mayor (Dr. Joseph), the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), the Chief Constable (Mr. Martin Nicholls), and the Director of Educa- tion (Mr. J. M. Murray) had made plans so complete and satisfactory that the day passed without the suggestion of a cloud to darken what must ever be a bright page in our local annals.</p>
        <p>His Majesty and the Queen could be seen and could see during the whole of the time that the Royal party was in town. The ljourney in an open carriage from A rpley to the Town Hall occupied a quarter of an hour, which was a literal interpretation of the instruction that we know was added to the list by Lord Derby that the carriage should drive " slowly " through the town, a thoughtful act Which the people will under- ptand and appreciate. At the Town Hall, at Messrs. Crosfield 's works, in fact, over every inch of the town, the utmost enthusiasm prevailed, and their Majesties left the borough with the knowledge that they have no more devoted subjects than those who toil on the borderland of Lancashire and Cheshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MR. ROBERT PIERPOINT REMEMBERED.</head>
        <p>MR. ROBERT PIERPOINT REMEMBERED.</p>
        <p>Among the personages who had special cause to be pleased with Me events of the day were Mr. and Mrs. Robert Pierpoint, who. were universally recognised and cheered. As 4 /natter of fact Mr. Pierpoint, who was in the citizens' procession from the bridge, after the Mayor had seconded the King's gracious act of formally opening it, was loudly cheered all along the route to the Town Hall. The frequent marked demon- strations forced back to the- mind of the writer vivid recollections of an incident which occurred in Warrington twenty years ago. At a Corservative gathering in those far away times, a speaker referred to Mr. Robert Pierpoint as their future candidate, or member, and the writer faithfully reported the statement which caused no little consternation in _official circles. The late Sir Gilbert Greenall was the sitting member, and letters came forth with the inquiry, "Under which King ? " How the matter was settled we do not know, but Mr. Pierpoint did become the member, and he has lived well and long enough to command a continuance of the lion's. share of the attentions of the Warrington people on such an. occasion as Monday provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>INVITATIONS TO KNOWSLEY.</head>
        <p>INVITATIONS TO KNOWSLEY.</p>
        <p>' Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Crosfield must be highly satisfied with Monday, also, for, during the presentations, Lord And Lady Derby invited them to attend the ehtertain- ment given at Knowsley the same night in honour of the King and Queen. Later in the week this courtesy was extended, for Alder- man and Mrs. W. Bolton were invited to Knowsley. As all Warringtonians know, the Alderman is one of the most successful growers of orchids in the country and on Monday he forwarded some of the choicest specimens from his wonderful collection to Knowsley for the acceptance of the Queen. On Thursday he received an intimation from Lord Derby that Her Majesty was anxious to thank him personally for the flowers, and Mrs. Bolton and he were asked to go to Knowsley Hall the same evening. They were most graciously rec3ived by her Majesty, who thanked them in cordial terms for the orchids, which she had greatly admired. Her Majesty also told Mrs. Bolton that she was delighted with the happy looks of the school children who were assembled in front of the Town Hall on Monday, and said the display was exceedingly pretty. Alder- man and Mrs. Bolton had the privilege of attending the cinematograph entertainment given at Knowsley in honour of their Majesties' visit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>No SUFFRAGISTS.</head>
        <p>No SUFFRAGISTS.</p>
        <p>We are glad that the uneasiness regarding a probable suffragist descent upon War- rington proved to be unjustified. Nothing of an untoward nature happened ; indeed the policemen who were imported from other towns for the occasion were loud in their praises of the Warrington crowds and looked upon their experiences here more in the light of a holiday than "special" Auty. Much of the ease with which. the people were controlled was due to the organisation of the Chief Constable and the barriers which he caused to be erected. It was the completeness of the system adopted in this respect that called for the favourable,notice of the King himself. The strange constables voted us the best town they had ever been to and hoped they would have as pleasant an experience for the rest of the tour.</p>
        <p>Mr. Murray and the teachers of the town who marshalled 10,000 young children on the Ip.wn before the Town Hall and saw them safely home again, without even a minor casualty, deserve perhaps the chiefest laurels of the day. It was a really fine accomplish- ment. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>NEW WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>NEW WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>The first of the three sections of the new Warrington Bridge, which was opened on I Monday, has been taken full advantage of during the week and has been a means of relieving the congestion at Bridge Foot that has been a greater danger than has been generally known for some time. We must push on now and get the other two sections completed and we shall then be in possession of the widest bridge in the provinces. It is not many towns of the size of Warrington that would launch upon a scheme for a bridge 80ft. wide, but there are not many towns with a crossing of a waterway upon which converge such important thorough- fares as Wilderspool, Knutsford and the New roads on the one side, and Bridge-street, Arpley-road and Mersey-street on the other.</p>
        <p>As to the name of the Bridge. The crossing at Warrington that has been the only link between Lancashire and Cheshire for centuries for miles around has been known as Warring- ton Bridge. Last week it was suggested in the Council that the Bridge should be called the King's Bridge, because the King was about to open it, but the Council very wisely turned a deaf ear to the proposal. North- wich and Knutsford people know what Warrington Bridge is, but they would be puzzled if they received any instruction con- taining the term King's Bridge. The name of, the old, vanishing bridge is Victoria Bridge, a recognition of the fact that it was opened in 1837, the year the late Queen came to the throne. But how many Warringtonians,even, would know what was meant if spoken to about Victoria Bridge?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ANOTHER WARRINGTON AUTHOR.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER WARRINGTON AUTHOR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wallace Gandy, author of "When Lionheart was King,' -a new book of con- siderable merit reviewed in to-day's "Examiner," is well-known to many of our readers. He is an old People's College boy and left Warrington ten years ago, London bound. Last year saw his introduc- tion to authorship, when Messrs. Macmillan placed his well-received version of a tale of old Persia in one of their series of English literature. Previous to this, occasional literary articles from his Pen had appeared in several periodicals, among them being "The Daily Dispatch," "The Clarion" and "The Schoolmaster." He has at present in the press some books designed for little readers, which will be among the first printed in accordance with the report of the committee on eyesight. They are "Uncle Remus," " Tennyson's Falcon and other stories" from Italian romances, and a selection from "King Arthur." All these are illustrated, and will shortly be published by Messrs. Sisson and Parker. Mr. Gandy's family still reside in Warrington, and Miss Gandy, his sister, is one of Mrs. Stansfield's capable assistants at Fairfield Girls' School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>RIGHT TO PURE AIR.</head>
        <p>THE RIGHT TO PURE AIR.</p>
        <p>There are signs that the way in which the evils connected with the smoke nuisance in Warrington are being brought to the notice of the public in the columns of the " Examiner " is bearing good fruit. Corre- spondence to hand shows that the seriousness of the matter is appreciated in responsible quarters, and all that remains to be done is that united action'on the parf of the Corpora- tion should be taken. A quotation from Herbert Spencer's "Social Statics" may •be useful at this juncture. He says :</p>
        <p>He who contanuinatesa the atmosphere breathed by his neighbour is infringing his neighbour's rights. Men having equal claims for the free use of the elements -having faculties which need this free use of the elements for their due exercise, and having that exercise more or less limited by whatever makes the elements more cr less inaccessible, are obvio-usly trespassed against by anyone who unnecessarily vitiates the elements and renders them detrimental to health or disagreeable to the senses; and, in its discharge of its function as protector, a government is obviously called upon t 3 afford redress to those so trespassed against.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>GREAT BELGIUM!</head>
        <p>GREAT BELGIUM!</p>
        <p>A Tariff Reform lecturer who held forth from the Conservative van at Cornhill on Tuesday night, said that Free Trade in this country had been "found and tried wanting." The quotation is usually put -somewhat differently, but after all one might as well mix up words as mix up figures, or to say "Yes—no" when one is asked whether Tariff Reform means food taxes. One thing this particular speaker seemed to be sure about was that he was pleading for Protection, an idea that is repudiated by his superiors who talk gliby of re-adjusting the basis of taxa- tion. He made amends, however, by falling into line by describing this miserable poverty- stricken land of ours after the orthodox Tory fashion,while he held up several nations as great and prosperous, and, of course, Pro- tectionist—including what? Great Belgium!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>IN A POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>IN A POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>Magistrate: "You are charged with break- ing a chair on this man's head." Prisoner: "I am very sorry yer Worship. I didn't mean to break the chair."</p>
        <p>AT MATINS. MONDAY JULY 7TH, 1913. I shall not see the King to-day Pass down the old grey streets I knew, Where joyous crowds their homage pay, And, fluttering, bend the favoured few. I may not hear the church bells pour, Their silvery ripple through the song, Nor the old curfew, ivof yore, Preach to a glad unheeding throng. In greyer streets, ungarlanded; Where royal pageants never come, I bow an unremembered head, ' And loyally keep cheer at home. But with the rest my prayer is made That clouds may pass and skies be blue, Even as we kneel the rains are stayed, And see, the sun is breaking through! ROSA E. GLADDIgG.</p>
        <p>Music in be Park. The Borough Band will play selections in the Park to-morrow (Sunday) afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, JULY 12, 1913.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>IN THE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>IN THE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>This week two of the most important Government Bills have been under consid- eration. Home Rule and Welsh Disestablish- ment—w ith Scottish Temperance—make up an entertaining programme and the progress made has been quite up to the mark from a Ministerial point of view. The third reading of the Home Rule Bill was carried on Monday by a majority of 109 ; the third reading of the Welsh Church Bill on Tuesday by a majority of 103. The Irish debate was perhaps most notable for the frank acknow- ledgement by the Opposition that they would meet the demand of three-quarters or four-fifths of the Irish people for self-govern— ment with sheer refusal and coercion. ' A minority confined to four counties is, in the view of the Opposition, justified in imposing a veto upon the desires of the overwhelming majority of the Irish nation, even to the length of armed resistance. And Mr. BONAR LAW became quite plaintive because the Irish people "will not accept the situation." An interesting note in the Welsh Church debate was struck by Mr. FRANCE, speaking for those Liberals who had last year sought to bring about a settle- ment by a compromise on Disendowment— an effort which Mr. FRANCE declared had only met with uncompromising opposition from members on the Tory side. There was really only one argument which was put forward by the opponents of the Welsh Church Bill. Mr. BRIDGEMAN, who moved the rejection of the Bill, contended that majorities are often wrong. That may be true as an abstract proposition, but it is impossible to advance from such an abstract proposition to the concrete claim that the Church of a minority should be maintained by endowments which were intended for a national institution, and by contributions levied by the State on a dissenting majority. Mr. LLOYD GEORGE made an unanswer- able reply to the argument that the disestab- lishment and disendowment of the Estab- lished Church in Wales would inflict blow upon religion. The Welsh people demand this measure, which they regard as essential to religious equality and to the removal of a stigma inflicted upon the religious life of the nation by- the establishment of a privileged position for the manner of worship which is peculiar to a minority. In the remotest part of Wales, Mr. LLOYD GEORGE said, a man had to go ten miles for a doctor; he had not to go more than two miles to a place of worship. Was it likely that a nation which had brought this about would ask for any- thing from the Legislature that would injure religious life? It appears to be an unanswer- able argument and the substantial majority on the third reading was a plain indication that the Government side of the House thought so too.</p>
        <p>DON'T LOOK OLD—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S KULPFIUR HAIR RE iTOßEß,which deepens to a naturalcolourin stew days.Lockyer's maintains a youthful appearance. Large bots everywhere. Post free 1/6. Bedford Laboratory, London,S.E</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>COUNTRYSIDE</head>
        <p>THE COUNTRYSIDE</p>
