      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>in Warrington,</head>
        <p>in Warrington,</p>
        <p>DEAL OF COUNTRY STILL LEFT.</p>
        <p>p Someone made the remark to me a few d a y - o E. in that" Wanington would be a nice place if 't, - ,ve toes -ar the country." I reminded my friend, who nnle tbst to the town once a month for business purposes, toe; there is still a deal of the country left in Warringures moreover, that while the town for beautiful fellow, cannot compare with, say, Edinburgh or Nr)ttieg a owing to lack of physical situation, it still ha oft, beauty of its own, especially when seen io tbei.sats grey twiiight, when all the hard lines and high,i/dps of have become subdued as by magic,,and the grottPle,e're of the buildings make a harmonious unity. The cee'r,rce the townie full of such groupings, and are a e°,7stio of inexhaustible pleasure to _those with the g at some future time, but for the present occasise"a• stone)., i mind and eye. I may enlarge on this invitin n confine my attention to evidence of the collar/ Warrington. Kingfisher in the Park. Within the bcundaries of the borough there alb/ over ninety-three acres of land, distributed ovelinon town, in use as parks and pleasure grounds. these the town cannot encroach. The eecond . .11,r5ed of these, covering seventeen and a half s"to,fe, in the very heart of the business centArf the --cisa Indeed, it can be said, that from the old Geererae; building in the midst, the quick, steady and vige.,,eot pulse of the town life is .controlled. As a freq:sis - visitor to this beauty spot, I feel grateful for its ei,"aor , of greenery'where one can sit down for half an " lo in the sylvan solitude and forget all about the ncige;) things of life,the slums (and all that the word inclu od within easy stone-throw, and the rush, bustle ens throb of the busy town beyond the trees. Ilere,„!:ere feels as in some quiet gallery in a great city, one can draw oneself apart and dream in the qtne— eh The other evening I took a leisurely saunter thre3it'o the gardens, and was agreeably surprised to se,be kingfi-her perching on a projecting bough neer 'co lake I watched it for ten minutes or se, but it , not move from its perch. This is not the first tiielkiag have seen one on the lake. Five years ago, l';',lsee through one evening with a schoolmaster friee"h'ing. saw one near the same place. It is very refres' indeed, to note that our favourite park has he,e°,oe sanctuary enough to give one of the shyest, as it ▪ oe of the prettiest, of our wild birds sufficient CO to take occasional part in our town life. Wild Flowers and Trees. eral It is pleasing, too, to renew acquaintance with-sec, -tee fl well-known wild Avers quite at home. An3ol4B"eis, rockery round the like red campion, yellow lice moneywort were in full flower, and in the water 001 cluster of the yellow water lily. On the artiu.eo- rockvrork of the exterior of the aquarium ths leaved toadflax grows gracefully in luxarious ance. Beek It is the trees that are interesting in ch Park and Town Hall ' Gardens;. not so teu-an their variety, as the abundance of the collo types. One does not expect to se3 our Beg nie forest trees flourishing in the heart of a 13°te,a,":o, lecturing town like they do, for instance, at "1-19, Abbey, but they do thrive with one or two exceptOgae evde in our carbon-laden atmosphere_ The, in evacuee of lal-ge elms with their rookery, whieTthe years gone by, were Buell a delightful feature ot 10 ge t'°„es trees, one, at least, still stands near the old gardens, no longer exist, and of the many lar cabin. A walk round the park will ehow that.at two species are not suitable to our town life, 128,133,„;;4 de hawthorn and the poplar. The hawthorn objects to a polluted atmosphere,. The black Pen is not a dedrable tree in any case It has features which make it objectionable.. It is one dirtiest native trees, and is peculiarly susceptible t▪ e insect and fungoid attacks. Why Trees Die. nd Being a rapid grower, it soon gets top beavY, b. is usually full cf unsightly dead and rotting braric,ler Smoke, although exceedingly dirty, is not alto,' the cause of these trees not prospering. There a other factors which may help. :Unsuitability 01,6„;ie is often detrimental, even under healthier atraosP`.7b conditions than obtain in Warziagton. Trees, w,ll bve't thick glossy skin on the upper surfaces leaves like the hol'y, laburnum, rhododen."'d • to hornbeam, plane ana ethers seem quite suitehioh our conditions, because the breathing pores It -tio are more numerous on the underside of the leavel of not get clogged up with the scot. A good shewe'•oo rain will speedily wash the leaves, at the ono ti supplying the,grcund with plenty cf plant fod of, frequent cause of dying trees, very often lost sight 00 is the occurrence of a very severe early winter afte„rate exceptionally wet summer and autumn. This ce'atel on an average about once in ten years, and roall.gtra- trees receive permanent injury, through the lieir ordinary amount of sap which is stored in ;root. tissues during a wet season being attacked by bt oligred Sometimes trees are killed by' what r be called accidental causes. A case of this °logic some years ago when all the rhododendrons in O. • Park were destrcyed in one night. The callsered an exceptionally dense fog which was heavily with acid fumes, followed by a severe frost. Birds Found in Warrington. bp Or Another realistic touch of the country iS the prising large number of birds which breed 113„e town or visit it regularly. There is a speciansee town birds in the Museum containing these, tu"thers species in all. There may be many which occasionally pay a visit to nay town, but thc set' exhibited are pre-ee3,Zoeg birds of the town. A large hawthorn tree, tc""„rtiee high !Treading branches, in the Museum g;iieo °pp:site the laboratory window, makes an ecierole observatory from which to watch the loiids- the there I have seen at least ten species of bircle—the omnipresent sparrow, the übiquitous starlingilirpy garrulous jackdaw, the chummy robin, the band blackbird, the song and missel thrushes, the some little blue tit, king chaffinch and JennYisabits, To write about their behaviour and hat while under observation, is tempting, _6,4 space will not allow. There are several very interttack. nesting sites worthy of mention. The colonel' °f taro claws on Wycliffe Church tower, for instance, th:fts at owl on Latchford Cantilever Bridge, and the s k'eries the corner of Crcsfiekt-street. There are no 1.00 bet now within the borough of which I am aware!, tbe several large ones exist on the outskirts, e;eily ia rooks are frequent visitors to the town, 01)9'13 the early morning. Last year I was informed, on what seemed authority, that the black-headed gulls had staries too nesting site on a likely spot in the town. It sca tien late to investigate the truth of the 0°2.1'3%180 at the time, but this season I have watched tue ;tore carefully and constantly, and while several aoci le, gulls in full breeding plumage are always aboo with them a couple of birds in first year's Plumio boite. can find no evidence of the existence of a nestingoticeel Whilst watching the gulls the other evening Ibe the a bird which on closer examination I found to 03 loowgivgs.. oystercatcher. There was no mistaking its bill, the white throat and white patch in the,,ird. IV Its walk, too, was characteristic of a wading "ir close was busy feeding and give every opportunity 10 observation. Rats on the March. . of rata leave One often hears in the cchntry of arm'ec... on the march, earcial'y atter harvest, when Enoh for their winter quarters, but it very seldom t 915 sgo an occurrence is reported in town. But two 77id that on the authority of-an eye witness, I Tv" tO3O uP, r,f,tle° an army of rats, several hundred in numbe tur Buttermarket-street in the middle of tbe nigui) where down Horsemarket, and into Golden squaraedc their history in lost. I wonder re if any relate a similar occurrence. G. A• DUNLOP.</p>
