      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Welsh Disestablishment.</head>
        <p>Welsh Disestablishment.</p>
        <p>The Junior Liberal Association has arranged a meeting in support of Welsh Disestablishment to be held at Bridge Foot on Friday evening next. Mr. John Griffiths will be the chairman and the principal speaker, Mr. F. Rhcdes Smith, of the Central (London) Campaign Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Cadet ,Corps and Royal Visit..</head>
        <p>Cadet ,Corps and Royal Visit..</p>
        <p>The Latchford Cadet Corps tooi part in the civic reception of tlieir Majesties cn Monday. Eight boys paraded, under the command of Sergeant-Major H. Kitchen, and were stationed in the park, along with the Church Lads' Brigade. They also joined in the procession to the new bridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Nonconformist Choir Union.</head>
        <p>Nonconformist Choir Union.</p>
        <p>At the annual festival of the Nonconformist Choir 7:nion held at the Crystal Palace (Ii Saturday, Miss Emily Taylor, of Bold-street Wesleyan Choir, War- rington, was awarded second prize in the contralto solo competition. The members of the Bold-street choir were to have taken part in one of the choral competitions, but, owing to the breakdown of a motor char-a-banc, which they had chartered to convey them to Chester, where they entrained for London, they did not reach the Me`ropolis until 350 in the afternoon, two hours after the competition had begun. Conse- quently they had to be content to play the part of listeners.</p>
        <p>Loyal Rejoicings at Appleton. Thera were loyal rejoicings on Monday at Hill Warrcn, Appleton, which forms part of the estate of Mr. T. IL Lyon. At about the time the King and Queen left Buckingham Palace, London, to commence the'r Lincasbire tour, a Union Jack was hoisted en a new flagstaff by the Hon. John Dalterg-Acton, the five-year-old grandson of Mr. and Mrs. Lyon. The National Anthem was played by th new Stre ten Brass Band, and cheers were given for the King and Queen. There was a, large attendance, including Mrs Lyon, Lady Acton and her chi'd en, the Rev. and Hon. C. F. Cross, and the Rev. W. J. Melville.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Parish Church Choir Trip.</head>
        <p>Parish Church Choir Trip.</p>
        <p>Scarborough was the venue of the senior members of the Parish Church choir cn the occasion of their annual trip last week-end. The party, Lumbering 22, left Bank Quay Station at 9 18 on Friday morning for Earletos,:n, where they joined the train which runs from Liverpool to Scarborcugb, a special saloon having been reserved. The famous and beautiful Yorkshire resort was reached at two o'clock, after a most comfortable and enj yable journey, The party made St. Nicholas Boat ding House their headquarters, where lunch was provided at 2.15, after which the visitors prcceeded on a tour of inspection, returning for dinner at G. 30. The evening was spent in a visit to the Spa and in bowling. On Saturday morning breakfast was partaken of at 7 30, after which the major portion of the party enjoyed a fine drive thrcrigh the richly wooded Forge Valley to Hackness, returning to Scarborough via Scalby just in time for dinner at noon. Tea was provided at four o'clock, and the homeward journey was commenced at ten minutes to five, Warrington- being reached at about ten o'clock. The arrangem,?nts, which had been made by Mr. A. Simkin, the hon. secretary, II era admirable in every detail.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Choir Excursion. The above choir held their annual picnic on Satur- day. Windermere was the place selected. Starting from Warrington at 7.15 the party arrived at their destination at, 11.15. An excellent dinner was partaken of, after which the party made tracks for Bowness pier, and boarded the steam yacht "Swift" for a sail to kmbleside. Arrived at this beautiful spot, a short climb was made up the hillside, from which a magnificent li.aw of the lake and surrounding mountains was obtained. About 5 o'clock the party walked back again to Ambleside, and made the return journey on the lake. Tea was served at G o'clock in Windermere, and then an Aber walk through the woods to the lake side was indulged in. This brought the day's programme to a closa. The day was delight- fully fine until 8.30 in the evening, then the rain came down rather copiously. Windermere was left at 9 o'clock and Warrington reached at 11.30. The Rev. T. T. James (ere pastor) and Mr. W. H. Payton (the organist) accompanied the party. The arrangements, which were excellent, had been made by Messrs. Dawson, who are the local agents of Frames, of Liverpool. Local Musical Successes</p>
        <p>Miss Isabel Dorothy Fleming has successfully passed the senior division in pianoforte playing of the Trinity College of Husic, London. Miss Eva Bennett obtained honours in the Intermediate Theory of Music Examination of Trinity College. Both are pupils of Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bic. The following pupils of Miss Maud Jennings, Wilderspool-camieway, passed very successrully at the recent examination held in Mancht ster : —Lily Taylor, Enville-street(thcory), certificate and bronze medqllist, having gained the maximum number of marks (100) ; piano, advanced senior grade, Sylvester Hall, Sankey- strect, 79 marks, Ist class; senior grade, Hilda Baker, Penketh, 82 marks, Ist class with honours; and May Mather, Church-street, SO marks, let class with honours ; intermediate grade, Evelyn Fraser, Stockton Heath, SI ma!ks, Ist claE s with honours ; junior grade Ellen Miller, Nora-street, 90 marks, Ist class with honours; primary grade, Alice Furnival, Bewsey-street, the maximum number of marks (100), with honours ; preparatory grade, Hilda Mary Dyke, the Causeway. 93 marks, with honours (she 13 only nine years of age; and Dennis Dulls', Stockton Heath, 88 marks, Ist class with honours. A Caravan Tour in Cheshire.</p>
        <p>Twenty-four members of the Guild of the Sacred Heart (St. Mary's C.Y.M.S.) experienced the joys of the "simple life " last week-end; when they enjoyed a caravan tour in Cheshire. Leaving the rooms in Dial-street cn Walking Diy afternoon they made their first stop at Stretton, where a cricket match and a gramphone recital enlightened the evening. On Saturday the party journeyed onward to Acton, where they encamped in an orchard until Sunday morning. Then touting through Norley and Crowton, they made for Hatchmere, a pretty lake on the edge of the famous forest of Delamere. Here a sports tournament was held, and each of the tents entered a team. The victors were No. 3 tent, captained by Bro. S. J. Price. A foned march vii Sutton Weaver and Daiesbury all 'wed the party to reach Warrington in gocd time on Tuesday evening, the experiment having proved most enjoyable. Mass was said each morning in the camp by the Rev. Father Mawson, chaplain of the society, and many local residents took the opportunity thus afforded of hearing Mass and attending the Sacraments. The whole of the arrangements for the tour were admirably carried out by Bro. William McHugh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>By Ship Canal to New Brighton.</head>
        <p>By Ship Canal to New Brighton.</p>
        <p>The annual excursion of the employes of Messrs. William Robertson, Ltd, Latchford, took place on Saturday. As was the case last year, New Brighton was the venue, and the Ss. Herald," one of the magnificent tenders of the Alexandra Towing Com- pany, Liverpool, was chartered to ei.nvey the party 'from Latchford Leeks. The beat left at about 645 a.m, with close upon 300 passengers aboard. The employes at the firm's Applct n works were embarked at Runcorn. The rain managed to keep ( if, although it was rather dull,and after a most enjoyable sail, New Brighton was reached about 10.15. The party made its way to the New Brighton Tower Grounds and sat down to an excellent lunch in the Algerian cafe. Mr. R. M. Monk, secretary to the company,read a telegram which he had received from Mr. Wareing (a director and works manager), who was unable to be pre• sent, as the marriage of his daughter was taking place that day. Mr. Wareing expressed heartiest good wishes for a very enjoyable day. The employes then drank the health of Miss Wareing and Tunstall and a telegram of congratulation was dis- patched to the happy couple. 1111. N. Cook proposed a vote of thanks to the management for the efficient manner in which they had carried cut the diningarrange- ments. This was seconded by Mr. Monk and carried. The afternoon was srent in and about the grounds, full advantage being taken of the various amusements. At 6.45 the party c:ossed by ferry boat to Liverpool, where they re-embarked on the Ss. "Herald" The return journey common -.ed at S o'clock and Latchford was reached at 11 o'clock, after an eminently success- ful outing. The journey, both to New Brighton and home, was acceptably enlivened by the capital music provided by an orchestra.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 12, 1913. THE RECTOR'S PROTEST.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 12, 1913. THE RECTOR'S PROTEST.</p>
        <p>Returns Tickets for the Civic Reception</p>
        <p>of their Majesties. CHURCH NOT RECOGNISED.</p>
        <p>The Rector (Rev. Canon Willis) did not attend the civic reception of their Majesties at the Town Hall,on Monday, and we under- stand that his absence was a protest against the ontission of the Reception Committee to recognise the Church in the ceremonies connected with the Royal visit. The list of townspeople presented to their Majesties did not include any representative of the Church of England, as such,or indeed of any religious denomination, and there was no form of religious service in Connection with the open- ing cf Warrington Bridge. Tickets entitling the Rector to seats on the stand at the Town Hall were forwarded to him,but he returned them to the Mayor with an intimation that, while thanking him sincerely for the invitation, he did not require any tickets. A representative of the " Examiner," who waited upon the Rector to inquire as to the motive which prompted his action, was informed that it was not taken on any personal grounds, but because he felt that, in view of the absence of all recognition of the Church on such an important occasion, he could not accept the tickets. His views on the point were concisely stated as follows in the current issue of the parish magazine :—" The Church of England is, as all well-instructed Churchpeople .know, the one and only true branch of Christ's Holy Catholic Church in this land. She's also the Established Church officially connected with the State as no other religious body is, and if ever there was an occasion on which her position should have been publicly acknowledged, it is this. when the Head of the State is coming to visit the town."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>"NO RECOGNITION OF GOD."</head>
        <p>"NO RECOGNITION OF GOD."</p>
        <p>Preaching at the Parish Church on Sunday evening, the Rector, in a reference to the Royal visit, said the King's private life was an exarnrle to them all ; his circle was a model cf affection ; the purity of his Court—which elicited the admiration of all—and his work for his people and his interest in their welfare was beyond all praise. Under these circum- stances their attitude was naturally one of deep respect, true love al d real joy, and they could respond to the admonition of his text, " Rejpice greatly; behol i thy King cometh unto thee.' At the same time he felt it to be necessary to express one great cause for regret, and that was the absence of all recognition of the Church on that important occasion. It did not matter who the representative was, but representation of the Church was clue, and .not only that, but there was to be an absence of all recogniti n of God in the ceremonies of the day. Liverpool was about to open the greatest dock that had ever been built, and a s-lemn service was to be held, at which the B:shop of the diocese would ask God's blessing upon the undertaking, but in War- rington, at the opening of the bridge, there was only a civil ceremony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>REV. JOHN YONGE ABSENT Because Religion was Vetoed Deliberately.</head>
        <p>REV. JOHN YONGE ABSENT Because Religion was Vetoed Deliberately.</p>
        <p>SLIGHT 0 N CHRISTIAN COMMUNITIES. [TO THE EDITOR OF THE "EXAMINER."]</p>
        <p>Sir—All loyal citizens of Warrington will have rejoiced in the recent visit of the King and Queen, and it is to be hoped that their Majesties will have carried away with them pleasurable memories of the occasion. The general arrangements made by our local authorities in connection with the great event,evidenced the manifold care which had been taken to render it, not only acceptable to the Royal visitors, but also to the com- munity at large. May I, however, ask your permission to point out in your columns one highly important detail which was not only omitted from the programme for their Majesty's reception, but, if current report be accurate, was censored by the committee having charge of the arrangements. No representatives from the clergy and ministers of religion in 'Warrington were included in the •list of presentations. I learn that a proposal for the inclusion of the Rector,a Non- conformist minister, and a Roman Catholic clergyman, was brought forward in the com- mittee, and was vetoed ! Had no such pro- posal been made, and its idea had not been discussed, there would be less occasion for criticism. But that it should have been made, and debated, and finally rejected, (rives the matter an importance which should be seriously noted. Finding this to have been the case, I personally felt I had no option but to be absent from the reception. The omission conveys a serious slight upon the Christian communities of our town. It is with much satisfaction that I learn that the Rector also withheld his presence from the Town Hall. Without endorsing the rather exclusive ecclesiastical views pro- pounded in his remarks in his Parish Magazine, I feel as keenly as he does the slight, I had almost used a stronger term, which is 'cast upon the Churches in Warrington, of every name, by the blank refusal of the Reception Committee to include any ministerial presentation to their Majesties. If the point had merely been overlooked, the omission had been less unfortunate. But for it to have been introduced, considered, and , refused. renders the omission exceedingly reprehensible. It might, indeed, be added to these criticisms that the omission of the " Mayor's Chaplain" from any recognition at this function was a failure in becoming decorum! The whole proceeding would have made inappreciable addition to the space allotted to the function, _ and both their Majesties and their loyal subjects would have been equally pleased.</p>
        <p>JOHN YONGE. 26, Ellesmere-road, July 11th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>STRIKING VIM BUK CURE.</head>
        <p>STRIKING VIM BUK CURE.</p>
        <p>Sores so Bad on Head that Hair Fell Out.</p>
        <p>" Our ycurgest daughter, Alice, aged 7, was sent home from school with ringworm on her head," said Mr. J. Carnall, of 3, Levens-street, Pendleton, Man- chester. "The child's hair fell out in bunches practically all over her head. Besides ointment from the school doctor, we tried home-made remedies, but Alice didn't get any better. "We took the child regularly for a year to the Manchester Skin Hospital. To our dismay, however, Alice's head broke out with itchy, inflamed places. So we dropped the hospital treatment and put the child under the care of private doctors. The disease continued to spread, however, and Alica was a really terrible sight. " Alice had been suffering quite two years without showing the least sign of ever being cured when we were pursuaded to try Zam-Buk. " Zam-Bult's effect was really astounding! The balm seemed to sink into the scalp and soothe the child's itchy, inflamed sores All bad matter was drawn away and the sores vanishel, being replaced by new healthy skin.</p>
        <p>"The School's Medical Officer passed Alice as quite cured, and she is now attending school regularly. We have a lot to thank ZAm-Buk for in our home."</p>
        <p>You can always depend on Zam-Buk as a sare and thcroughly reliable skin balm. It has a unique herbal compsition, is skilfully manufactured and always maintains the same high standard of healing value. To always have Zam-Buk handy is to save money and worry.</p>
        <p>GARDEN FURNITtRE, TENTS, HAMMOCKS, CHAIRS, S. GARLAND, 51, 53, arid 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>TAXI-CAB FATALITY.</head>
        <p>TAXI-CAB FATALITY.</p>
        <p>Child Killed in Knutsford-road on</p>
        <p>Walking Day.</p>
        <p>Conflict of Evidence.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, held an itqairy at the Warrington Police Station yesterday, concerning the death of Annie Snutch (11), daughter of William Snutch, gas range fitter, Oxford-street. Warrington, who was knocked down by a taxi-cab in linutsford-road on the evening of Walking Day, as she was returning from a Sunday school treat in Wilderspool Park, and sustained injuries from which she died in the Infirmary. Tne car was drirsen by Walter Beresford, manager for the Warrington Taxi-cab Company, Lion Hotel garage. —Mr. F. H. Swallow appeared for Beresford, and Mr. Arthur Browne for the relatives of the deceased, while the Chief Constable watched the proceedings on behalf of the police. The evidence showed that while crossing the road- way the deceased stooped to pick something up. She then went forward and was knocked down by the car, which had suddenly swerved to the right. There wEs a confl:ct of evidence as to the rate of speed at which the car was travelling.—Mary Ann ‘Vinstanley, I, Fairfield Place, said it was going far too quickly, considering the narrowness of the rod. William Brown, horse driver, ISS, linutsford-road, said that when the car passed his house he remarked to a frlend : " He will kill somebody before he gets to Warrington Bridge." No sooner had he uttered the rema.k, than the accident occurred. The Coroner : What made you say that ?—Because I thought he was going very, very quickly and that, as it was Walking Day, there VI oula be a lct of people in the side streets.</p>
        <p>Witness estimated that the car was travelling at from 35 to 40 miles an hour, and that it did not come to a standstill until it had gone another 40 yards. Thomas Connolly, carter, 7, Nicholsor-3treet, who was in company with the last witn,ss, said the accident would have been avoided if the child had returned to the footpath. Be judged the speed of the car at from 25 to 30 miles an hour.</p>
        <p>Frank Wilson, cab driver, 214, Koutsford-road, said the car was travelling at a " pretty fair " speed—about 20 miles an hour. If the driver had kept to his left- hand side he would never have touchel the child.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Kenwright stated that after the accHent he went to the Infirmary and asked Dr. Law, senior house surgeon, his opinion ts to Beresford's state when he took the deceased to the institu'ion. He stated that he was " rational enough." Later, in company with Inspector Forster, witness stw Beresford. He was under the influence of drink but could not he termed drunk. Beresford denied that he had driven recklessly but said : "Of course, there are times. even when you are well used to driving, when you drive faster than you think you are doing." The Chief Constable: Da you think Beresford would, in his condition, have been allowed on the railway to take charge of a locomotive ?—Certainly not. They would have sent him out of the shed instantly. Thomas Jackr.on, gas main layer, St. Mary-strect,put the speed at from 15 to 20 mi:es an hour. He thought it would be reasonable on the driver's part to E.ssume that the child would return to the footpath on the left hand s'de of the road. If she had done so, the car would not have struck her. Witness had no reason to think that Beresford was drunk or incapable of driving a car. Under the circurnstances,the speed was excessive. Joseph Brierley, natal broker, 279, Kntr,sford-road, described the speed as very dangerous. James Thomas Lee, 29,Thelwall-lane,said he remarked to a friend that, if the car went on to Warrington bridge at such a speed, it would kill someone. He put the speed at from 35 to 40 miles an hour. The Coroner said that in his opinion the evidence was such as to justify him in telling the jury, without hesitatipn that no jury would convict Beresford of manslaughter.</p>
        <p>Walter Beresford was called. He stated that the child sucHenly ran into the roadway and stooped to pick something up. Thinking that she would return to the footpath, he swerved the car to the right and decetsed was knocked dcwn. The car was not travell- ing at above 12 calks an hour, but he could not pull up immediately owing to the slippery state of the road. He did not agree that the car went 10 3ardB before coming to a standstill. James William Warburton, Ash Mile, Albert-road, Grappenhall and Frank Hickman, Hazlewood, Latch- for.i Without, who were travelling in the taxicab, stated that the vehicle was going at a "fair speed." They put it at from 16 to 18 miles an hour and:frora 16 to 20 miles an hour respectively. The road was quite clear until the deceased steppei dr the footpath. Beresford appeared to have had some drink but was sober and quite capable of taking charge of the car. The jury returned a verdict that the accident was unavoidable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>TRIBUTE TO A TEACHLR.</head>
        <p>TRIBUTE TO A TEACHLR.</p>
        <p>Memorial to the Late Miss J. E. Hetherington.</p>
        <p>A very touching and yet pleasing ceremony took place at the Beamont Council Infants' Semol, on Wednesday afternoon, when a portrait of the late head mistress, Miss J. E. Hetherington, was presented to the school. This memorial is the gift of the Educa- tion Committee, and bears the following inscription : County Borough of Warrington. Education Committee. MiLs Josephine E. Hetherington Head Mistress of tte - Cockhedge British Infants' S,-..h001, 1881-1894. St. Gorge's Mission Irrfants' S3hool, IEOI-1907. Beamont Council Infants' School, 1907-1912. _ _ .</p>
        <p>Born April 14th 1818. Died October 6th 1912. Several relatives and friends of the late Miss Hether- ington were present and also the Rev. Canon M. Stevenson (Chairman of tte Manager 3), Messrs. J. Fulton-Smith, J. M. Murray (Director of Education), B. B. Roberts (Assistant Director), and W. Mason (Head master, Bailment Council School), Miss M. Taylor (Head mistress, Girls' Department) and the scholars of the Infants' Dapartment.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Council]. ,r 11. Rob3rts, Chair- man of the Education Committee, regretting his absence, but expressing his entire sympathy with the object of the gathering. The Rev. Canon M. Stevenson, in explaining to the children the reason for their being thus assembled, recalled the many long years of faithful service whichthe late mistress had given for the benefit of the young people of the town. He spoke of the beautiful example her life had been—one of service and sacrifice—and hoped that for many years to come the memorial he was about to unveil would remind them of their departed friend and mistress. He then asked the infants to sing the hymn, " I think when I read that sweet story of old" (one of the favr 'trite hymns of the late Niles Hetherington), after which he unveiled VA) portrait. Miss E. Cooke (headmistress of the Infants' Dspart- ment), expressed the thanks of the staff and scholars for the beautiful and lasting memento with %Lich they had beFn presented and Nhich wr.uld be to them a source of inspiration to try to do their work as cheer- fully and patiently as she had done, and which showect that in Warrington, at least, the work of 1113 teacher is respected and honoured. Mr. J. Fulton-Smith, in replying, asked the children to remember that this was a portrait" of a good woman, who always thought of others before herself." The proceedings closed with the singing of the hymn , "On the Resurrection Morning," beautifully and touch- ingly rendered by selected boys and girls from t'_ie senior departmentF.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE continued. " George V. Commemoration Shields.</head>
        <p>LOCAL INTELLIGENCE continued. " George V. Commemoration Shields.</p>
        <p>The" George V. " commemoration shields awarded to Arpley-street (girls') school and Parochial (boys)' school for proficiency in athletics were presented to the winners by the Mayor, just before the arrival of the King and Queen at the Town Hall on Monday. The shields, which were supplied by Mr. F. Hallett, silversmith, 10, Bridge-street, are of special design, in solid silver, mounted on polished oak. The centre carries the County Borough Arms, in rich enamel, with ornamental ribbon above suitably inscribed, and 12 smaller shields around for engraving the names of the winning schools.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Knighthood for Mr. J. S. Harmood Banner,</head>
        <p>Knighthood for Mr. J. S. Harmood Banner,</p>
        <p>M.P.</p>
        <p>The visit of the King and Queen to Liverl)7°l yesterday was marked by the conferiing of a knight- hood upon the Lord Mayor (Mr. J. Harmosci. Banner, M.P.), who is one of the mot interesting figures in the nublie and commercial life of Lancashire. Sir J. S. Harm- ood Banner is chairman of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Co.. Limited, and in recogni- tlon of the signal horour done to the head of the con- cern, flags ',vete displayed at the Warrington works and ofliccs of the company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Hallway Staff Picnic.</head>
        <p>Hallway Staff Picnic.</p>
        <p>The clerical stAff connected with the goods depart- ment of the London and .Noi th-Western Railway had their annual outing to Rhyl on Saturday. Arriving at the North Wales resort at 11 a.m. th 3 party had dinner at the Belvoir Hotel. Afterwards a 30-mile motor tour was taken to Llanfairtalhaiarn, the route being through the ancient town of Rhuddlan, with its ivy-grown castle. On arrival at their destinaticn—a picturesque village nestlirg amidst the mountains—a ralt was made, and the party indulged in a ramble an the mourtains. A start was made for home via the famous Bryn-y-pinn Pass, termed "The Switzerland of Wales." On reaching St. Asaph the Cathedral was inspected, after 1, hich, the party set out for Rhyl, and on arrival did justice to a splendid tea provided at the Belvoir Hotel. The return journey was made by the 7.50 p.m. train.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WEDDINGS.</p>
        <p>Furnival—Bracegirdle.</p>
        <p>The wedding took place at Friars' Green Chapel, Cairc-street, on Thursday afternoon, of Mr. Harry Furnival, e!dest son of Mr. and Mrs. W. Furnival, Bewsey-street, and Miss Bessie Bracegirdle, eldest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. D. Bracegirdle. The bride was prettily attired in a white satin gown draped.with pink roses and hat to match, and carried a bouquet of carnations and lilies. The bridegroom's present to the bride was a gold watch bracelet. The bridesmaids, Misses Alice Furnival and Vere Bracegirdle, wore dainty saxe blue poplin dresses, trimmed with lace. with hats to match, and carried bouquets of sweet peas and smilax and wore pretty gold bangles, the gifts of thedbridegroom. The ceremony was performed by Alderman James Evans, the bride being given away by her u-cle. Mr. W. E. Wright acted as best man. Mr. W. Birch presided at the organ and played suitable wedding music including Lohengrin's 'Bridal March."</p>
        <p>The wedding breakfast was provided in the new schoolroom, about SO sitting down, after which an impromptu concert was held, items being furnished by the bride and bridegr om,Mr.J.Petts, (Manchester), Miss Alice Furnival and Mr. Herbert Jolley. Later, the happy couple left for Ballycastle, where the honey- moon is being spent. The bride w ore a grey travelling costume, with a black hat trimmed with pink roses. The presents were numerous, and several congratu- latory telegrams were received.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Tunstall—Wareing.</head>
        <p>Tunstall—Wareing.</p>
        <p>The picturesque ancient church at Grappenhall was the scene of a fashionab!e wedding on SAturday, when Mr. Sidney Laverock Tunstall, son f the late Mr. Thomas Tunstall, former ly of " Brookside." Penketh, and Mrs. Tunstall Belvoir," Lattho:d, was married to Mss Gladys Wareing, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Fred 'Wareing, "The Popl irs," Grappen- hall. Both parties are well known in the district, and there was a large congregation to witness the ceremony The cfEciating clergy man was the Bev. Harry Roberts, M.A. (vicar of St. Matthew's, Ilkotle, uncle of the bride) assisted by the Rev. H. V. Figott, M.A. The service was fully choral, and Mr W. Asquith Woods., who pre- sided at the organ, played Mendelssohn's "Wedding Match" in an impressive matner. The bride, who was given away I y her father, was attired in a dress cf soft ivory satin, with long square train, dacorated at the bottom with orange blossom and myrtle. She also wore a bodice of Brussels lace and a veil of Brussels net over a half circle of erar ge blossom, and carried a sheaf of white lilies. Toe bridesmaids were Miss May Waring (sis..ter of the bride), Miss Tunstall (sister of the IA idegrcom), Miss P. Tunstall (cousin of the bridegroom), and Miss Gerty Neil. They koke d charming in dresses of shell pink crepe-de-chine with figured heliotrope nincn, and black picture hats trimmed with rose's shaded to match the dresses,and black streamers. Their ornaments were gold bracelets, and they carried sheafs of pink roses, the gifts of the bridegroom. Mr. Leonard Pierpoint was the best man. After the ceremony, a reception, at which 50 guests assembled, was held at the 'The Poplars," where the usual toasts were honoured. Later in the day the newly-married couple left for Scotland, where the honeymoon is being spent. The bride travelled in a tailor-made costume of apricot cloth with white 'hat veiled with ninon and lace. On their return Mr. and Mrs. Tunstall will reside at Hill ClifTe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"Only an Artist's Model."</p>
        <p>Something out of the ordinary run of melodrama is seen at the Royal Court Theatre, this week in the play 'Only an Artist's Model," which has been successfully presented by Mr. Stanley Carlton and his excellent company. The story of the country girl who goes from her humble cottage to a world of gaiety in London, her life as a model, her desertion and sub- sequent happiness, is most realiAtically enacted, and the interludes, both sensational and humorous, are painted and bright. Miss Ruby Maude i full of dash and vigour as Alice -Mas n, the model, whose love of diamonds, silk and lace, coupled with an innocent longing to see life, brings her so near a pit fall. Mr. Stanley Carlton,who is a powerful actor, bas a difficult role to play in V.ctor Ballard, the artist, whose ambition for fame leads him to actions which earn con- demnation. Whilst receiving the execrations of the "gods " in the major pAlion of the play, he is greeted as a popular hero when be makes • re' aration to Alice. Mr. Edward Cudd makes up and acts as the hypccritical Rupert Slim, who causes much laughter with his " Yea, verily." Miss Isabel Marte gives a homely characterisation of Mrs. Mason, whilst Miss M. Leslie Cudd acts well as Diana Barclay, a passionate and jealous lover. A wealth of comedy is suppied by Miss Ruby Lee as Belle Lpraine, and Mr. William Annesley as Peggy Fairfax, the dude. The other characters are well sustained and the play is full cf grip, the court scene being an intensely exciting concluding item. NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>The great detective drama "In a man's power" or, "Nick Carter in society," will be presented next week by Mr. J. Forbes Knowles and Mr. Harry Masterrnan's Co. Miss Mary Lovett will appear as "Cynthia."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME</p>
        <p>Mr. A. Turpin, the enterprising manager of the Hippodrome, is to be congratulated on organising such a ragtime singing cimpetition as we have had this week at the popular Barbau'd-street house. Mr. Turpin's object is to try and discover any latent talent in our midst, and in a measure he has been successful, for some of the artis'es are really clever ragtime singers. There is a ladies' and gents' section, but, as Mr. Turpin has announced each night, ladies are rather backward in competing. There are two sets of prizes of 4:3, ,:k.".2 and £l. The finals were conducted last night, and the winning com- petitors u ill sing in both houses to-night (Saturday). The names of the winners appear in the local news column. The tarns this week are also above the average. The Child Trio are the cleverest juvenile musicians we have seen for some time, and were loudly applauded fr their pla3ing of the R iymond overture. Poland Bros, are smart and well-set athletes, who poifortn the most daring fea!s. Jarvis and Martine present a capital novel comedy juggling act, being expert club swingers and throwers. " A queer customer" is the tile of a highiy diverting act by Johnson and Bert, who are character comedians, with a complete knowledge of how to create laughter. Ray's Football Girls are smart skaters, and score a success in a novel and sensational football match on skates. Gladys Huxley is a charming comedienne, and Lucy Coventry sings catchy songs and dances exceedingly well.</p>
        <p>NEST WEEK'S PROGRAMME GEORGE FORMBY.</p>
        <p>The great La-n-c-pshire comedian, George Formby, will appear at the Hippodrome next week. Formby achieved another success at the command performance at Knowsley Hall, last 'Monday, and his powers as a laughter provoker are well-known locally. Other turns will include :—Francis and Alfred, jugglers ; Caradini, act,r musician ; Pete and Minnie Le, in Scotch canedy ; Renee Ray, in beautiful artistic tableau ; Ellis Barrieton, chorus singer ; and Marie Pascal, ia wonderful feats on the six-feet pedestal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>THEATRE ROYAL.</head>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL.</p>
        <p>The picture season at the Theatre Royal, Scotland- road, is proving v.ry popular, and large houses have witnessed a capital programme this week. "Moths" is the title of a highly interesting film, which will be shown to-night, along with •` Carmen" and other excellent pictures which have been displayed since Thursday. NEXT WEEY_'S TrsiIOGRAIIME. The principal picture for Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday is "The Seeker" (in three parts), which is a graphic story of a 3 oung man's rise to fame and the evil results of his throwing over good friends for new false ones. On Thursday, Friday and Saturday the chief films will be "Great Possessions "(in three parts), a great human drama, and "A dead man's secret," a drama of the seamy side of life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>PICTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>A great attraction has been secured by the manage- ment for next week, in the shape of an exclusive film of the tour of the King and Queen through Lancashire. Th:s film, which is an exceptionally fine one, was shown by Mess's. Weisker Bros., to their Majesties, at Knowsley Hall, on Thursday evening, by special command. It will be projected at every performance next week. In additim " A Crown Prince's Love Story" will be shown on Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, and" Lying Li pa" on Thursday, Friday, and Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>ftttrts fht abitar</head>
        <p>ftttrts fht abitar</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>THE EDITOR of the "Examiner" invites correspond- ence on all subjects of public interest.</p>
        <p>LETTERS should be brief- and pointed. Their object. must be to impart or seek information, not abuse. LETTERS must in all cases be accompanied by the name and address of the writer. COMMUNICATIoNs received early in the week—except urgent matters can always be given the best attention.</p>
        <p>BUCKLEY-STREET SCHOOL COT COLLECTION. Sir,—On behalf of the officials of the Buckley-r,treet Sunday School I wish to express heartiest thanks to everyone who assisted in making the cot collection so decided a succeEs. We asked for a record collection ; the ever generous public of Warrington fittingly responded and we got it. The exact amount received was .4:9 3s. id., which slightly exceeds any previous effort, and this only a week after the inspiring results of Alexandra Day !</p>
        <p>A. TAYLOR, Su pei intendent.</p>
        <p>AMBULANCE WORK AT THE KING'S VISIT, Sir,—As an enthusiastic National Service Leaguer, I was pleased to see the Red Cross women, marching up to the To,yn Hall, as I thought, to make themselves generally useful. I was, ho ever, shocked to see th4m (by command) march up to and take their seats on the stand as spectators. In tl;e circumstances it was a farce to be dresied up in the Red Cross Society garb. Some very sarcastic remarks were made, eveu by military friends. I am informed that the ambulance people had seven members of the Preston Nursing Division to assist them (some wearing the South African medal), in addition to about 15 of the Warrington Nursing Division, and that they (the ambulance people) had to treat about 200 cases of fainting, and over 2,000 drinks cf water were-given in your sanitary drinking cups, mostly to women and children. Even some of the Territorials were treated. A NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>DRINK AND POVERTY.</head>
        <p>DRINK AND POVERTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Mr. Taylor begs the question altogether in his reply to my criticism of his address, iu which he attemp•ei to illustrate "How Drink causes poverty," by taking a district peopled mainly by th,se in receipt of regular wages and c mpaling it with a district inhabited by large cumbe.s of casual labourers.</p>
        <p>The man with regular wages will always be com- fortably off in comparison with those only earning two to three days' pay per week ;ad sometimes none at all. It matters not whether there be a large number of public-houses or only a few in a district, for the individual who drinks to extremes, will as readily manage it whether there be one or a hundred. Surely that will be easily understood. That only a very small proportion of poverty can be, attributed direct), or indirectly to " drink " is affirmed by every unbiassed authority, and it is the greatest disservica to the poor to attempt t lead people to believe that it is their fault and not (as it is undoubtedly in the majority of cases) their mis- fortune. But Mr. Taylor is an extreme teetotaller, and, therefore, can only see the extrema point of view. May I remind him that temperance is moderation in more things than alcoli 1? It is temperance in argument and in illustration, in act and thought. I do not refer to his figures as to the proportion of wages paid in the manufacture of liquor (Mr. Tucker is no doubt quite able to deal effectively with them), excepting to remind Mr. Taylor that there is scarcely any business or trade that does not benefit indirectly, to an enormous extent, by the brewing and distilling industry, and that Mr. Taylor is relieved of a large share of taxation that he would otherwise be compelled , to bear if it were not VI- the huge proportion very readily and acceptably received by the Government of this country. July 3rd, 1013</p>
        <p>G. E. HOLME.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SOVEREIGN FOR A PENNY.</head>
        <p>A SOVEREIGN FOR A PENNY.</p>
        <p>The following anecdote, for which we vouch being true, shows how the scepticism often ce sts a lcss. Two noble Lords discussing this subject decided to test the matter and made a bet to settle it. One wagered to stand disguised as a pedlar on London Bridge with a tray full of golden sovereigns, and to offer them for sale at cite penny each, for a full hour without finding a customer. This, strange as it may seem, was done, but the public were too sceptical to buy, and the bet was won by one of the noble gentlemen. Here was a chance for the man in the street to obtain 19/11 for nothing, but because no one was sensible enough to examine the sovereigns offered at a penny— a splendid opportunity was lost by the folks who passed and smiled instead. Had the noble Lord who posed as vendor ut up a guarantee that the sovereigns were genuine, probably his tray wculd soon have been emptied. Now Messrs. Suits Ltd , of llorsetnarket-street, Warrington, claim to produce a suit to measure for 20s. that is equal to a 40s. suit purchased elsewhere. Moreover, they ( ffer every investigation of their claim by the public before they buy, and GUARANTEE that their garments will tit perfectly and wear well and very satisfactorily. Here, therefore, is a chance for all sensible Warring- ton men to investigate before he spends his hard earned money, to see if he cannot obtain 40s. worth of value for 205., or in other words, save 20s. by judicious forethought. Messrs. Suits Ltd. guarantee of absolute satisfaction or money back represents honest trading, and, if their organisation permits them to cffer the public their high grade Tailoring at rockbottom prier s, the inhabitants of Warrington and diAriet will be wise to at least keep that additional sovereign in their pccketS and visit 17, Horsemarket-street, Warrington, Messrs. Suits Ltd.'s establisinrent, before going elsewhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>JUDGE'S'IMARNING.</head>
        <p>JUDGE'S'IMARNING.</p>
        <p>A Notice Which Was a " Gross Misrepresentation " of the Law.</p>
        <p>His Honour -Judge Stanger, E.C., at the Warrington County Court on Thursday, uttered a serious warning against the sending out to debtors of a notice which he described as a gross misrepresentation. It will be remembered that his Honour passed some strictures on the document at a previous Court, but deferred further comment in order that the person in whose name the notice was issued—Robert C. Pearson, of Eldon-street, might have the opportunity of appearing befole him. The notice, which was printed on blue paper, read : " Final notice (in large letters), before (in smaller letters) proceedings in the County Court (in large letters). I hereby give you notice that unless the rum of 10s. 6d. due from you is paid, I shall cause proceed- ings to be taken against you in the County Court." There was also what purported to be extracts from an Act of Pazliament.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Pearson, who stated that she managed the business in respect of which the notice was issued, appeared on Thursday. She told his Honour that she bought several of the notices from a man who charged 93. a dozcn for them, and who said he had sold a great m,ny of them. She was rather short of money, and consequently sent cut three of the notices to people who owed her sometLi2g. His Honour said he must warn Mrs. Pearson very seriously that she was incurring grave risk by sending out such a notice. Probably she did not know, but it came very near to being a criminal offence. Although the notice did not, in terms, profess to come from the County Court, it was drawn up and printed in such a way that an ignorant person might conclude that it was an olncial document of the Court. Moreover, the alleged extracts from an Act of Parliament were gross misrepresentations of the law. The Act, in question, was repealed nearly a quarter of a century ago, but, even then, the extracts misrepresented the law as it formerly stood. That raised the question of trying to obtain money by false pretences. If he found that such papers were being used again, concluded his Honour, he would consider whether it was not his duty to send them to the Director of Public Prosecutions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Farm Strike Settled.</head>
        <p>Farm Strike Settled.</p>
        <p>The strike of farm labourers in West Lancashire ended on Monday, a committee of six farmers and six labourers, with an independent chairman, having been farmed to make a speedy settlement of the question of hours and wages, and to notify all farmers that a general stoppage should be observed on Saturday a t two o'clock, to commence to-day.</p>
