      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>WHERE THERE ARE NO POOR</head>
        <p>WHERE THERE ARE NO POOR</p>
        <p>A writer in " Industri3.l Canada" has just completed a tour which included the important manufacturing cities and towns of Western Ontario. In every one, he states, the skeleton timbers of factories and additions to factories were rising rapidly. Factories, long established and prosperous, were installing new machinery. The signs 'Help Wanted" weie numerous. Pr,cessions of loaded freight cars were moving out of switches. Many manufacturers stated that they could not keep up to their orders. There seemed to be no idle men anywhere. Manufacturir.g suburl. s, full of workmen's homes, were appearing in places where the hayfields rustled a few years ago. Good homes they were, too, and, walking among them, one was forced to wonder if there were any poor. The factories are humming, and their employes are getting overtime cheques in their pay Envelopes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>INSURING GOOD DIGESTION.</head>
        <p>INSURING GOOD DIGESTION.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANCE OF PREVENTING FOOD FERVENTATION IN STOMACH.</p>
        <p>Good digestion usually means good health. So long as the stomach does its work properly disease can find no lodgment in the human E ystera ; the liver, kidneys and intestines will perform their work perfectly and remain in prfect health ; but interfere with the proper digestion of fo3d and an unending train of troubles is set in motion. About the only thing tnat wi'l upset the stomach and interfere with the digestion is an excess ( f acid, usually caused by food fermentation. Food fermentation is due to chemical action in the food itself. This fermentation causes the formation of gas, or wind, as well as acid, resulting in the unnatural distention of the stomach and the burning by the acid of the delicate lining of the stomach. Medicines and tonics cannot relieve this condition— they usually make it worse. The fermentation and acid are to blame, and these must be overcome and prevented by taking after meals half a teaspoonful of bisurated magnesia in a little hot or cold water. Physicians recommend bisurated magnesia, especially because it stops or prevents fermentation of food and neutralises the acid instantly, making it bland and harmless, without irritating or upsEtting the stomach in any way. Get a little bisurated magnesia from your chemist and try it the next time your food ferments and upsets your stomach. Note how quickly the acid is neutralised, and how soon you forget that yr.o have such a thing as a stomach.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PENZANCE.</head>
        <p>PENZANCE.</p>
        <p>At Penzance and throughout the Cornish Riviera the most glorious weather has prevailed of late, and a steady influx of visitors is noticeable. The inaugura- tion of a summer steamship passenger service between Penzance and the Scilly Islands has provided an addi- tional pleasure for visitors to this delightful spot. The trips to and from Scilly on Mondays and Fridays from July 25th to September 3rd will be cheap day excursions, without distinction cf class, at the popular fare of 4s.</p>
        <p>grow rapidly and make up into very nice table chickens. A READER'S IMPRESSIONS. - -</p>
        <p>As I write, a note is dropped through my letter box from a reader who was there. Mr. G. G. Gill, Penketh, writes : _ . .</p>
        <p>"The things I was most struck with were the absolute simplicity of every detail, which, successively arranged on a premeditated scale, worked to a finality. of perfection in so short a time. Never have I seen so healthy small chicks or such fine pullets and cockerels 12 weeks old. The absence of forcing foods, the menu being a p'ain sound dietary of every- day foods of the best quality to be gr.t. proved to me with the greatest satisfaction what could be done without condiment?. The number of large chickeLs in one house (60) would suffocate in an ordinary chicken house. But under this fresh-air system all are in excellent condition." Mr. Gill's letter goes to show how favourably Mr. Poynter's system impresses a practical mind. And what I have said before is supported here by Mr. Gill. However much naqy know, theoretically or practical!y, one has to admit that there is a great deal to learn from a visit to Haslington Hall. SMALL-HOLDER CO-OPERATION.</p>
        <p>Our tour of inspection ended, we wended our way back to the fine old Hall, there to be received with equal cordiality by Mrs. Paynter. At the hands of our hostess we were the recipients of a welcome cup of tea. An interesting conversation on the possibilities of the future of poultry farming took place. Mr. Paynter is a great believer in the co-operative idea am-)ngst small-holders. As he very ably pointed out, this work is not the work f the lich man, it is not a work which can be done by the capital at, inasmuch as the one great essential to success is individual interest in the actual work on the farm. .</p>
        <p>A b-tter system of education with the application of scientific and systematic methods is what is required to make the poultry industry a success. Side by side with the butcher's shop, Mr. Paynter would like to see a shop where fowl flesh would be supplied. But until we can hope to get this we must first pro- duce the flesh. The hands of the clock hinted that we must now make our way homeward. This came all too soon, and the three hours and a half spent at Haslington Hall last Saturday will be a pleasant memory, to those who took the opportunity of going, for many long day. I took it upon myself to thank Mr. and Mrs. Paynter for all their kindness in making our visit a pleasant one ; and also the editor of the "Examiner," for what he had done in helping to make our little trip enjoyable. This was most heartily supported by everyone present. Space prevents giving the impressions of other readers. These however will appear in next week's "Examiner."</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>INTRUDER CAPTURED.</head>
        <p>INTRUDER CAPTURED.</p>
        <p>Story of an Exciting Encounter near Warrington. The story of an exciting struggle with en alleged burglar was related at the Warrington Occasional Court on Tuesday, before Mr. George Mather, when Charles Smith, of no fixed abode, was charged with having broken into and entered the dwelling-house of Mr. William Price, retired excise cfficer, " luglewood," Poulton-with-Fearnhead, with intent to steal on the 3rd July. Mr. Price stated that at 10.30 p.m. on Thursday of last week he retired to bed, leavini., the windows and outside doors locked. At 3.30 a.m. be was awakened by a noise downstairs. He looked over the bannisters and noticed that the doors downstairs were open. He called out," Is that 300, Mary ? " thinking that the mH wcs perhaps moving about the hou3e, but received no answer. having armed himself with a spear, he went downstairs, but found that nothing had been dis- turbed in the drawing-room. The drawers and cupboards on the sideboard in the dining-room had been opened, and a spirit decanter an 4 other articles were on the table. He then rushed into the kitchen and found that a large window-pane had been broken. Cn looking out of the window he saw the prisoner standing near the backyard gate. On seeing that he was discovered, prisoner made off to the front gate. Rushing through the house, witness • overtook him and pinned him to the ground. Prisoner was in his stockinged ftet. Mrs. Paco went for Mr.l Joseph Benrett, who promptly came upon the scene, helped witness to bind the prisme-, who was afterwards handed over to the police. He had effected an entrance by prising a window sash with an axe (produced), which was the property of Alderman Bennett. Prisoner had nothing in his rossession, and witness had not missed anything. He thought that prisoner had drunk some of the port and whisky. Prisoner said he was drinking all the afternoon with a pensioner who" treated "him. He went to sleep in a Oleic' and awoke in the night," muddled and half drunk." He had no distinct recollection of what after- wards happened. He came to Warrington on the Tuesday to look for work, and lodged in Upper Bank- etreet. He should ha7e gone there to sleep on the Thursday night, if be hal not got muddled. Priscner, who had a long list of previous convictions, was committed for trial at Liverpool Quarter Sessions. Smith was further charged with a similar offence at Paddington House, Poulton-with-Fearnhead, the mai- nence of Alderman Bennett. Prosecutor stated at 7 a.m. on the 4th inst., he went downstairs and found that a window in the breakfast room had been forced open. The axe (produced) was his property. There was a mark on the window ledge, and footprints under the window. A desk and sr,me drawers had been opened, and one of the curtains bad been pinned back. P.S. Clarke stated that in answer to the charge prisoner replied, " I don't remember." Prisoner said he was in drink and did not remember the occurrence. Smith was also committed for trial on this charge.</p>
        <p>YOUNG CHILD RUN OVER. A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned at St. Helens on Saturday, at an inquest on the body of Winifred Agnes Harris, eighteen months old, of 138, Elephant-lane, Thatto Heath, who died on Wednesday afternoon from injuries received through being run over in the street. The mother said she saw the child run out and she followed, but before she got up to her a passing cart had gone over her. Enoch Jolle3, carter for the Co-operative Scciety, said his horse a as walking 'slowly and he was riding on the cart. He saw the child on he footpath, and later someone ehouted that his cart bad gone over her. He hurried cif for Dr. Cotton, lut the child died stout two hours later.</p>
        <p>oid iabar CHICALA A DRY FEED FOR CHICKENS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>AGENTS</head>
        <p>AGENTS</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON, BRIDGE ST. WARRINGTON.</p>
