      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>POULTRYMEN'S PILGRIMAGE</head>
        <p>POULTRYMEN'S PILGRIMAGE</p>
        <p>" Examiner" Readers have a Profitable Half-day at Haslington Hall.</p>
        <p>THE VALUE OF SYSTEM.</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP] TEe trip to Haslington Hall is now a thing of the Satutday last saw a small hut very interested party of poultry men en route for the scene of action. On arrival at Crewe Station we hired an open convey- ance and then drove through three miles of the most beautiful Cheshire country that one could desire. This brought us to Haslington Hall, to be most cordially received by Mr. F. G. Painter. No time was lost. At "Mr. Punter's suggestion we started our journey of investigation in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>INCUBATOR ROOM.</head>
        <p>THE INCUBATOR ROOM.</p>
        <p>Here we saw the mammoth egg machines heavily laden with latent life. There was no shyness exhibited by our party, and the result of the friendly cross-examination which tcok p`ace, is a sure indication that all of us came away with far more knowledge of artificial incubation -than we ever knew before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>LAMP ROOM.</head>
        <p>THE LAMP ROOM.</p>
        <p>The next department visited was the lamp room. Here our hcst explained in detail the work of filling and trimming the array of lamps which are used to beat the foster-mothers. Much useful advice was given as to their construction and price. We were ‘certainly let into every secret and every detail; the result of a most valuable pracfcal experience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>FOOD ROOM.</head>
        <p>THE FOOD ROOM.</p>
        <p>We next made our way to the food rocm. Readers ,of this column will remember that I went rather fully ibto what takes place as regards the mixing and making-up of the foods at Haslington Hall some time ag,. But those who saw and heard for themselves, will best be repaid when practical application is made with one's own little stock of f ,wls at home. Mr. Paynter's patience is generosity itself, but it took him some little time to get the " Examiner" researchers out of this most important department of the works. At last we sallied forth to see one thousand eight hundred healthy young chickens in the field near by. Here we saw the actual product of Mr. Paynter's unceasing toil—birds from mere baby chicks a few days old, to what looked like 53 many over- grown youths, ranging about - a vast contented family. The dint ent pens were commented on, as well as the different crosses from which they had been bred. Perannally, I was pleased to see that a better class of fowls bad been produced as a result of Mr. Payn- ter's search for the right kind of eggs for incubation. The Faverolles—Sussex were in great evidence, and ,this cross is highly praised at Haslington Hall. They</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, July 12, 1913</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 12, 1913</p>
        <p>DAYLIGHT ROBBERS.</p>
        <p>A Good Harvest During the Royal Visit at Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>DEPUTY MAYOR'S LOSS.</head>
        <p>DEPUTY MAYOR'S LOSS.</p>
        <p>Thieves took advantage of the Royal visit to Warrington, on Monday, to break into several houses on the outskirti of the town.</p>
        <p>Wh,le Alderman Forshaw, the deputy Mayor, was taking part in the reception of their Majesties at the Town Hall, intruders were busy ransacking his residence, Ivy Mount, Latchford Without. An entrance was effected by forcing a back door, and when the family returned they found the house in a state of great disorder, the depredators having clone consider- able damage, as well as appropriating some valuables. The articles missing included a diamond stud, a diamond tie-ring, which was presented to Alderman Forshaw by an old friend when he was elected Mayor of Warrington, a diamond pin and stud belonging to his son, Mr. W. B. Forshaw. Altogether, the Alderman estimates his loss, by theft and damage, at about</p>
        <p>Ile house of Mr. William IW-)erts, Harledene, La'ch- ford Without, was also broken into while the family were witnessing the ceremonies at the Town Hall. The contents of drawers in two bedrooms were scattered about in all directions, and the loss is estimated at between Y._,10 and Y-12.</p>
        <p>A robbery took place at the laLus3 of Mr. John Forrest, of Penley, Latchford Without, jewellery and money of the value from ,1.:15 to S-20 I)iug takeu. A TRADESMAN ROBBED.</p>
        <p>Pickpockets were busy in the streets, and Mr. J. W Hall, hz tter, Mark, t Gate, was relieved of £l3, in a particularly impudent way. He W_S bolding his child at the top of a step-ladder, which he had placed a few yards from the side door if his shop, in ordei that the youngster might have a view cf the procession. He (Mr. Hall) was sanding on a lower step when a stranger walked up to him, said the position f )r both was dangerous, and offered to steady the ladder. Mr. Hall raised no objecti ,n, and when the Royal proces- sion plesed along Sankey-street on its way to the Town Hall he discovered that his pocket had been picked of £l3. The s•ranger had, of course, made off. ARRESTED ON SUSPICION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Police Court Sequel to Monday's Great Throngs.</head>
        <p>Police Court Sequel to Monday's Great Throngs.</p>
        <p>As could only be expected, the light-fingered gentry wero busy amongst the huge crowds which assembled in Warrington on Monday, on the occasion of the Royal visit. The police made three arrests of suspected persons, and a sequel was heard at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, when, before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair) and other magistrates, Joseph Flynn, William Evans and George Murray were charged with loitering in Bridge-street with intent to commit a felony. In asking that the prisoners should be remanded, Superintendent Williamson said that at three o'clock the previous afternoon Detective-Sergeant Kinley and Detective Robson, of he Liverpool City Police, were near the Seven Stars Hotel in Bridge-street, when they saw Flynn loitering in a suspicious manner. Coitered by the other prisoner and two more men, he was endeavouring to put his hand in a gentleman's hip pocket. Flynn was arrested in Mersey-street by Detec- tive fanley and slid," Give us a chaac I have done nothing yet. I have only been here ten minutes?' The officers continued their soarcb, ard et 3.20 Evans and Murray were seen near Market Gate. %here a man had just had his pocket picAel of £l4. Prisoners were counting money. after which the police lost sight of them. At 4.15 Evans was seen in Winmarleigh-street, when an offi;er arr-s:ed him. He exclaimed, " Why, I hive done nothing." When chi red at the Police Station he remarked, "1 will make you sit up for this."</p>
        <p>Inquiries were continued, and it was ascertained that seven men had each deposited a bag at the parcel office at Bank Quay Station. At 5.15 two of the men had been to c'aim two of the bags and the polica kept watch. Later on Murray preEented tickets for the remaining hags. When Detective Summers and Clough arrested him, Murray was very indignant, and endeavoured to get away. He threw himself on the ground and struggled desperately. Murray was seen by Sergeant Kinky and taxed in regard to being in Bridge-street. He replied, " Yes, I was there this afternoon. 1 came back for the bag. What cin I do when lam out of work ? I have a kick child at home and must do something." Fl3nn had £3 15s. 6d. in his possetsion, Evans had £l4 and Murray £4. :Flynn said that the statement which the Supvrin- tendent had read out was hot evidence. What chance were they giving him? He did not know the other two men. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Chairman : It is a statement in justification o tie Superintendent's app'i ation for a remand. Pr;Eoners were remanded tor seven days.</p>
      </div>
