      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Lesson from General Booth's Life.</head>
        <p>A Lesson from General Booth's Life.</p>
        <p>The wet weather made it impossible to hold Sunday's meeting of this society out of &amp;ors, as had been arranged, and the usual meeting in the church was presided over by the Rev. T. T. .lamer. Mr. E. Wynne Jones gave the address on "Quit you like men." Meek- ness, he raid, did not mean a meekly man, but he received Etrength by a reliance upon the strong arm of God. He had no fear of man, but a strong fear of offending his God. The speaker urged the men to try at all times to do the right, and to be the right. Meek- ness was a conqueror daring to do for God. It met with opposition, but ultimately brought the reward. General Booth and other leaders of men had to receive scorn and ridicule, but the General lived to be acknow- ledged by the monarchs of this and other lands. He hambly waited and trusted in his Gcd. Wycliffe Band was in attendance and led in the tinging.</p>
      </div>
