      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>IN THE COURTS</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS</p>
        <p>PAWNSHOP ROBBERY.</p>
        <p>Window Broken and Goods Appropriated.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. J. Fairclough (in the chair), and other magistrates, Edward Clifton, hawker, P;g Hill, James C'Brien, Pig Hill, and Edward Mullen, of n) settled abode, were charged with having been on the enclosed premises of Benedict Tyrer, pawnbroker, 100, Buttermarket-street, with intent to commit a felony at 11.50 p.m. on the 26th ult. They were further ()barged with stealing 24 rings and two pendants, value £2, the property of Thomas Domville, pawnbroker, 67-69, Church-street, between the 26th and 27th ult. . _ _</p>
        <p>Superintendent Williamson said that prisoners were seen in Mr-. Tyrers' yard by Mrs. Atherton, Smith- street. She called out and one of the men %eat to her and raid," What have you seen ? We only want a Lit of stuff out of that shop." A neighbour was called and prisoners made off, two of them going to Manchester bra a motor lorry. They returned the next day to 111,3 Police Station and asked what they were being wanted for. Wnen told, they admitted the offence. At 12.33 a.m. on the '27th ult. it was found that the front window had been broken with a stone and the goods taken. Mullin bad offered rings for sale the next day. A man named Mulligan bad said, " You want locking up for selling them at that price, when Mullin offered him a ring tr r ls.—Mullen stated that he knew he was a bad character. His convictions were many, but he hoped they would not prejudice the magistrates in the present case.—On the first charge, O'Brien was sent to prison fir two months. Clifton was fined 20s. and costs or ono month, and they were dismissed on the second charge. Mullen was committed for three months on each charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. B.:3'ore the Mayor. (in the chair), Alderman James Ev.ns, Messrs. %V. Furness, G. Pilling, S. Wright, W. Roberts, A. J. Jolley and C. S. Burgess. WOUNDING CHARGE.—Henry Darlington, of no fixed abode, was charged with cutting and wounding an Irish labourer named Thomas Donnellan, by strikirg him on the head with a pick shaft or similar weapon. Super- intendent WilEaroson stated that Ihnnellan lest his way cn Central Station at 12 o'clock on Saturday night. He wandered al.,ng the line until he reached a plate- la3ers' cabin. He sat down arid prisoner who had come frcm beh nd the cabin asked him for rconey. Prisorer then wect round the cabin again, and on c '' oming back struck Darnellan six blows on the head with the heavy stick. Donnellan struggled along the line until he reached Padgate in an exhausted cm- di t ion, where he was seen by a doctor. About 4.50 a.m., rn Monday, P.O Gibson saw the prisoner wandering aimlessly about Horsemarket- street. Taxed by the officer, he said he had struck a man on the head with an iron bar and did not know whether he was dead and did not care. When charged he replied, "That's right." A broken pick shalt wis found near the cabin by a police officer. Donnellan was taken to the Workhouse Infirmary. A number of stitches had to be put in his bead and he had lost a large quantity of blood. In answer to the Mayor, the Superintendent sti.ted that prisoner was sober when brought into the police station at 5.10 a.m.—Darlington was remanded for seven days.</p>
        <p>DANGEROUS DRlVlNG.—Percival Hill, a Widnes cab- driver, was charged with having been drunk whilst in charge of three horses and a waggonette in Sankey- street, on Saturday night. It was stated that at 10.20 p.m. prisoner was trying to drive the vahicle over Back Quay Bridge, but failed to do so. When reepeated by an cfficer to dismount from the box, he ne.rly fell. The vehicle, containing 30 passengers, stuck in the tram lines and traffic was greatly obstructed. Superintendent Williamson srld it was a wonder no one was killee.—Plisoner said be was sorry.—Tte Mayor sail it was a serious offence. The danger was to other people. As long as the dricer of a waggonette was addicted to drunkenness the people whom be drove were in danger.—A fine of lOa. and costs was imposed with an alternative of 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>W EDNESDAY.</head>
        <p>W EDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. J. W. Hayward (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Messrs. C. Jackson Holmas and W. T. Williams.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED SAVAGE ASSAULT.—IEaac Smith, 10, Manley-street, was charged on remand with having inflicted grievous bodily harm on Robert Lee (61), of no fixed address, by striking him in the eye with his fist in Priestley-street, on Friday. Superintendent Williamson said that on the previous day, Lee was coming from the direction of Froghall Bridge and was walking along Priestley-street. He was wearing glasses and had taken them off to wipe them when he happened to t( uch prisoner. Smith, who was much the worse for drink, dealt the old man a savage blow in the eye with his fist, knocking him to the ground. Lee had only one e3e and prisoner knocked the eye-glass into it, and it was doubtful whether or not the sight of that eye would be lost.—Prisoner alleged that Lee asked him for one penny and then knocked him down.—The cum vvas adjourned.—On Thursday the injured man was able to give evidence, and Smith was fined 405., 15s. going to compensate Lee.—Dr. Law, ronior house surgeon at the Infirmary, said that Lee's injuries consisted of a dislocation of the lens, the result being that the man's sight was seriously impaired. It was doubtful whether it would ever be better as the lens was twiuted. In his opinion a slight degree of violence might have precipitated the dislocation of the lens.</p>
