      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>AMONG THE WORKERS.</head>
        <p>AMONG THE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Their Majesties' interesting Visit to</p>
        <p>_ Messrs. Crosfield's Works.</p>
        <p>WORKPEOPLE.</p>
        <p>with her, expr,ssed his admiration if the vocal efforts of the girls. He appeared to be under the impression that they had been specially trained to sing for his benefit, and was surprised and pleased to learn that they were accustomed to sing while at work. He then asked how often they sang, and Miss Marsh replied, "Twice a day," adding that the object was to relieve the monotony of their work. His Majesty remarked upon the beautiful voices of the girls and the sweet- ness of their singing. Their Majesties' attention was called to bottles containing attar of roses and ambergris - a sweet-smelling substance obtained from whales. Then Miss Willett, one of the workgirls, advanced, and, with a charming curtsey, handed the Queen a casket in which were gold-stoppered bottles of scent. One of the perfumes "Gloria," had been made expressly for the Queen, and the other blends are known as "Rose of England " and " Lilac of England." Her Majesty accepted the gift with a smiling "Thank you." The King was the recipient, at the hands of Miss Willett, of a souvenir in the shape of three tablets of soap. Presentations were also made to the ladies of the suite. At a table in one corner of the room a number of girls were engaged in the work of lining the nickel-plated cases in which shaving sticks are enclosed, and their Majesties watched them with marked interest. They left the rooms to the strains of " Cherry Ripe," delightfully sung by the workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CHAT WITH THE OLDEST WORKMAN.</head>
        <p>A CHAT WITH THE OLDEST WORKMAN.</p>
        <p>Their Majesties were next conducted to the unorna- mental and strictly practical region of vats and soap pans. At the entrance of the soapery an inter- esting family group was in waiting. It consisted of George Dodd Weave, the oldest workman of the firm, his three sons, four granddaughters and two grandsons, who, between them, aggregate 180 years service with the firm. Mr. J. J. Crosfield at once introduced Mr. Weave to the King,who cordially shook bands with him. Mr. Gleave was, of course, delighted with this recognition, and, in a self-possessed manner, related to his Majesty the leading features of the life story with which readers of the " Examiner " are already familiar. His Majesty inquired as to his age, and, on Mr. Gleave replying" Seventy-two, " he com- mented, " I am glad to see you looking so remarkably well." " And I feel well, too," said Mr. Gleave. He then went on to tell his M ijesty how his father before him worked f)r the firm from its foundation in 1815, and, having lost his sight in the course of his employ-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <p>CHATS WITH f 0 la°o3 the pomp and circumstance of civic ceremonial actual contact with the " daily round " of the it°rlters • oee striking transition, but arrangements had th_ sel It made whereby their Majesties could see for to.7.laves at ej, one of Warrington's leading manufac- 01%es se quarters. The inspection of the works srs• Joseph Crosfield and Sons,Ltd., was a happy roadtseloation of the Royal visit, and their Majesties ittele:° secret of the fact that it afforded them ss pleasure. Of course, three quarters of an Ctirß ia a Very short time in which to gain even a eoe-°rY acquaintance with the numerous processes eluected with soap-making, and their Majesties spent °110! a few moments in each department. They were, able to follow the evolution of soap from de• boiling ingredients in the huge iron pans to the wistYfisished articles which merrily singing girls ets Placirg in cardboard boxes. representative of the " Examiner," who bad the tit:lileße of accompanying the Royal party through °works, writes : A miII°WDED THREE-QUARTERS OF AN HOUR. so;°re crowded three-quarters of an hour must fall to the lot even of Royalty than that which l`hir l'fai'sties spent in Messrs. Crosfield's works. eY listened to explanations—necessarily very con- Itillthed—of a variety of processes, chatted genially workpeople about their conditions of employ- tleenit.,, listened to the homely and touching recital of ikea Ite story of the oldest employe, accepted hand- cl'Et: souvenirs, and traversed no inconsiderable 11 .ttcein their tour of the many departments - and liiBpileclPs than the specifiei time. It was a rapid e01`,1,13n, and yet it sufficed to give their Majesties a Tenensive view of one of the many industries eu bring grist to the mill in Warrington. AN INSPIRING SCENE. LIRPI • . th^ ring and full of colour was the scene outside of works where a great multitude awaited the arrival r: their eatie ajesties. For hours people had stood 4ttlY b mehind the barriers, at what was considered whiloi the best pints of vantage on the whole route, It the„tPee employes of the firm who were not engaged ttiea," uePartments to be inspected by their Majesties liiet'sred at full strength in the works yard to give a bffi Y Lancashire greeting to the august visitors. The flie,es and adjoining buildings looked beautiful in feeit festal array. Floral garlands hung in graceful O 5 0008, and flags of:many nationalities were fixed to (10;taental shields. The recesses ab ,ve the office rbiek.ew,a37 were artistically treated with trellis arches, the (Mit in roses, while a large crown symbolised Pro firm. the favour about to be conferred on the irm. Whiel, the entrance to the edge of the footpath, at the Royal carriage was to draw up, was a gariPY awning prettily draped and hung with "pl:flud,s• Toe entrance to the works yard was by a golden trellis arch emb-llistied h irophri-. plants. The fire station was gay with froti, et 8, fligs and festoons, streamers being suspended o toe all centre mast on the roof. the PPcisite the offices a guard of honour, consisting of I)arecr,roPany of Territorials in the firm's employ, vevu under the command of Captain A. B. Layton, eel Ws manager. The famous brass band 4ered the crowd with choice selections, and the lke firlßade and ambulance corps were stationed near read.re station. In Factory-lane motor cats were in kuult'e,s to convey their Majesties and suite. to ltiv,Vl(-Y, a R yal standard distinguishing the valuei e the, tett the King and Queen would travel. From lhel? f adjoining buildings p-ople looked down on Neefigri_uY effective ensemble, and amusement was the antics of one man perched near the chreY stack of a house. Contingents of scho I,oeit!'en from the rural districts were allotted lil);`lena on Bank Quay Bridge wheie they had every rtY and comfort, consistent with safety. At THE ARRIVAL. 4r' lb o'clock the boom of guns signalised the ' E,rel'aval of their Majesti s in Warr n;ton and the air Loe tile charged with excitement. A quarter of an herr later and the sound of distant cheering was chitilde °n the breeze, the slirli acci.aims of the :*ell massed in front of the Town Hall being quite keel: heard. Next the waving of flags and hand- fiisfs on the bridge told of the near approach of °Yal visitors and the mounted escort was seen Nr,ling over the crown of the bridge. Then the open llotviage, 'and their Majesties graciously smiling and `he'llg their acknowledgments of the delighted dre:,,afts " which welcomed them. As the carriage NIA ",P at the office entrance, the guard of honour biNeu 'and the band played the National Anthem. NthediatelY their Majesties bad alighted, Lord eoei introduced Mr. J. J. Crosfield (chairman of the broßl3fiatlY), and Captain G. R. Crosfield. Mr. J. J. 'aslae'd then presented Dr. Markel and Mr. Cyril 1118 jilt (directors), who wee to assist in condirtting Zal party through the *works. In the entrance 41.8. IVEL J• J. Crosfield, Mrs. Haslam and Mr. and P. Jones were presented to their Majesties. Ii SIGNING THE VISITORS' BOOK. :'ftltit:iin.F, ascended the carpeted staircase, the first act fit•Cie-r4.41-j'eties was to sign the visitors' b ,ok thus, pleg-ifel "Mary." Then they passed through 'Dries-feral offices, where clerks were waking valiant kbicle_j l° appear absorbed in ledgers while stealing 'llterc.'ng glances at the Sovereign and his Cons ,rt. the 1;19g the laboratory their Majesties were shown R,041:4101t5 raw materials which go to the making of l'it.t!eand the King especially seemed to 1)1 keealy in the scientific side of the industry. vvELCOME FROM THE WORKGIRLS. Il'itirt?„ssing the bridge which leads to the 401 it 1,-,tg room their Majesties were, for the "le, visible to the great crowd assembled in</p>
