      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>CHILDREN'S GREETING.</head>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S GREETING.</p>
        <p>Ten Thousand Youthful Enthusiasts Cheer their Sovereign.</p>
        <p>BOY AND GIRL COMMENTS.</p>
        <p>Some Interesting and Out-of-the-way Criticisms.</p>
        <p>The smooth and spacious lawn sloping upwards to, the Royal dais has never been put to bettefuse than to provide a vantage ground from which ten thousand youthful enthusiasts could greet their Sovereign. Some thoughtful child lover, mindful of the longer- living mein 'ries of the young, had suggested that the choicest position should be reserved for the school children, and the Reception Committee of the Council had wisely invited the skilled experience of the Director of Education and representatives of the teachers of the town in making the necessary arrangements. 'I he results secure for all time that where children are concerned this co-operation will always be sought. For thanks to the perfect organisstion and the consideration of every possible emergency the marshalling and management of this stupendous body of children proceeded- like an everyday function and without the slightest mishap. From all the ends of the town processions of joyona mites wended their ways by carefully plotted routes and each, timed to minutes, fell into its allotted space. Every child of the ten thousand had an unobstructed view, everyone a coloured pennon hidden away ready to be produced at a pre-arranged signal, and everyone a psnt-up vocal welcome waiting only the first glimpse of the beloved rulers to rush out into a very riot of tumultuous cheering. The waiting minutes went quite quickly. First the practices of the National Anthem, made necessary by an eleventh hour dictum of Lord Derby; then the procession to the dais of the Mayoral party and the ladies and gentlemen to be presented. What a chatter there was , as the little ones spied the rich apparel of the Mayor and his mace bearer! One little fellow introduced the latter as "That's 'im —'ve king ! " to his little neighbour, and she declared " 'e's carrin"is crown on a stick ! " _ _</p>
        <p>Another little loyalist asked his teacher," Will the King and Queen have their crowns on ? " and seemed so disappointed that "they'll only be dressed like fav'er and muvver ! " Then followed the presentations of the "George V." shields to the bays of the Parochial School and the girls of Arpley-street, and be it noted that the thousands of little sportsmen of both sexes spontane- ously cheered the victors—there's no jealousy inside the schools. Similar cheers were given</p>
      </div>
