      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <p>ment, was sent t-) aLi verpoll instituti )11 to learn how to make mats and baskets.</p>
        <p>It was the prpudest day of George Gleave's life, and he cheerfully submitted to the ordeal of "inter- views by a dozen or more journalists. Asked as to his impressions of the King,he remarked, with obvious sincerity, " He is a very nice gentleman, and very free." The Queen, he added, was charming, and she grariously moved to him and smilcd. Meanwhile, the Queen had been chatting with Mies Cl ira Gleave, one of the granddaughters, and inquir- ing as to the working hours of the girls. "How lorg h rye ou been here ? " her Maj.sty asked, and on eceiving the reply, "Seven years," she said, "And this gentleman (Mr. (leo. Gleave) Ins been here sixty- three years."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>PLEASANT SURPRISE</head>
        <p>A PLEASANT SURPRISE</p>
        <p>Their Maj sties experienced a pleasant surprise during their tour of the soapery. One side of the department opens on to the river, and, desirous. no doubt, of enjoying the fresh breeze, their Majesties advanced to the open window. They were at once cheered to the echo by crowds of people who lined the banks, and they also saw part of the firm's fleet, gaily dressed with bunting. KING JOKES WITH MR. N. HOUGH.</p>
        <p>Mr. Nathan Hough, who superintends the firm's transp-irt work at Liverpool, had the honour of an introduction to his Majesty. "This is our Captain, who has served forty-five years, with honour," said Mr. J J Crosfield, whereupon his Majesty warmly shook hands with Mr. Hough. Next the King asked Mr. tiough how many steamers and barges the firm had on the river, and Mr. Hough rep'ied, "Ten,your Majesty." Fhe King followed this with an inquiry as to where the vessels plied, and Mr. Hough replied, "To Man- chester and Liverpool, where they deliver goods which are forwarded to all p-irts of the world." His Maj-sty then asked if the transporter bridge, of which a good view is obtained from the soapery, was used for F )ot passengers only, or for other purrises as we! 1. Mr. Hough replied that it was mainly used for trans- porting g.iods and was working alm-ist night and city. Turning tl Mr. Hough, his Majesty then said, : " I suppose you have often had a lough time 'going down the Mersey ? " and Mr. Hough responded, "I have, your Majesty. But it is not so bad now that we have steamers, as when we had sailing craft." This answer seemed greatly to please his Majesty, who laughingly observed, " I expect you have been ashore many a "Your Majesty is right," replied Mr. Hough. The veteran c iptain was then presented to the Queen by his Mtj ,sty and Captain Crosfiell Shaking hinds with him, her M ijesty congratulated Mr. Hough len looking so well at his age (he is in his seventieth (year), and expressed the hope that he would live for many years: .</p>
        <p>l'sjiug through the experimental laboratory, their Nlajesties paused to admire a fountain of soap</p>
        <p>THE KING TALES TO A WORKGIRL.</p>
        <p>In the piper bag room there was another of those little incidents which go to show bow anxious their Majesties are to familiarise themselves with the work ~f their humbler subjects. A bright girl named Polly Cotterill was busily engaged in folding ti e paper bags s they emerged from an ingenious little machine. It was not the clever mechanical device, but the human lenient which at once arrested the attention of the K;ng. " Bow many bags do you turn out per day ?" asked his Majesty. " Between 12,000 and 13,000 a day,' was the prompt reply. " And doesn't the work hurt your hands ? " was the next sympithetic inquiry. " No, your Majesty, I am used to it, " was the shy respors,-. The Queen spoke to Mr. William Tynan, foreman of the department, as to the capacity of the m 'chines, and, on being told that they made 100,000 bags per day in the department, she remarked that it was wonderful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>ANOTHER VETERAN.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER VETERAN.</p>
        <p>Returning .to th.e 7rapping room, their Majesties heard the girls singing "Sweet and low." As the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>HOW THE SCHOOL CHILDREN WELCOMED THEIR KING AND QUEEN.</head>
        <p>HOW THE SCHOOL CHILDREN WELCOMED THEIR KING AND QUEEN.</p>
        <p>TOE KING AND THE CHIEF CONSTABLE,</p>
        <p>The King did not omit in express his great satie- faction with the police arrangements. Before his Maj-sty entered the c-ir, ly)rd Derby presented the Chief Constable of Warrington (Mr. Martin Nicholls), who had accompanied the Royal party through the works, and the King hid a short conversation with him. His Majesty complimented Mr. Nicholls in unstinted terms on the excellent provision made to ensure the safe progress of the Royal prccession. Be said the arrangements were most satisfactory in every respect, and he expressed his special admiration of the barricading of the streets. When their Maj sties had taken their places in the car, Mr. J. J. Crosfi.lc'd called for three cheers, the request being enthusi st'cally complied with. Toe lust glimpse the multitude had of their MAjesties wai of the King raising his hat and the Qu-en smiling graciously in response to the loyal plaudits. -</p>
        <p>Private P. Wooltn. S ,rved in 88th and 00th Foot. Medals: oiim9an, and clasp (Sebastopol). Private P. Dannett, 138 linutsfo-d-road. Madras Fusiliers (joined in 185 G). Mutiny medal, clasp, Luck now.</p>
        <p>Staff Sergeant J. McGarr, Orford. Served in 16th Royal County Downs, 34th Regiment. Private R. Hough, Nicht)lsm-street, served in 99th Foot Wiltshire Regiment. Nledals : China with clasps for Pekin.</p>
        <p>ivate Yuile, 46, Slater-s'reet, served in 3 d Bomb iy Eurocem Regiment. Joined ]SI. Solved in Indian Mutiny. Medal : Indian Mutiny, easp Jbansi.</p>
        <p>Private J. Kehoe, 50, New road, left the King's (Liverpool Regiment) in May, 1879, after 21 years'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
