      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <p>to the champion boy and girl, and the only envious mmilent came when the fl ;ck of press photographers shot their shutters at the champions. " They'll be on at the pictu les," was a general ixclamation, and really a little erriy could be detected in the tone. The photogniphs of the3e v!ctors in the field of athletics have already appeared in the "Examiner." At last, a boom of exploding shell told the eager youngster's that the Royal train had arrived, and with one consent the crowded lawn turned right about and saw the disseminating smoke; then a" Oh," and a " one, two, three, "and thousands of voices counted in audible to :es through the series till " twenty-one " t Id how Carr ctly the counting harl been followed. For the first time the flutter of expectancy was noticeable in the throng,and little hands sought for the gay red, White and b'ue. What eagerness at last ; and to the beating of the whole of the guardisn teachers, "God save the King," in excellent time and splendid tone, rang from the genuine hearts as well as the lips of the children.</p>
        <p>A sea of co!our waved its welcome as the Royal carriage turned into view, to the accompaniment of a scream of cheers that must have Ouched the hearts of the Royal Parents as they caught their first glimpsn at the living picture. Then came the craning necks as each child peered to get a closer view of the Sovereign and his gracious Consort, and a babel of comment that no scribe could justly record. From the girls : ',lsn't she beautiful," as the Queen smiled upon them ; and "Isn't be brown," as the boys saw the King's well-bronzed countenance. The ceremony cf presentation was closely watched, and the pressing of the butto i whiCh-opened the bridge quite generally understood. As the Mayor called for the cheers which came so readily, the departing monarchs were again closely scrutinized and a myriad heads slowly pivoted to watch tie disappearing carriage. Just a shade of disappointment was seen in the faces of the tiny mites on the edge of the lawn, and this disappointment was crystalised in the speech of a seven-year-old :</p>
        <p>66 11(3.1:lever SPOKE to t;E. ! "</p>
        <p>Then back with swinging steps to their respective schools they went, cheered by the memories of a burst of glorious sulshine on a threatening day, and full of a living loyalty to the figure heads of their beloved fatherland.</p>
        <p>That ten thousand little folks should have assembled in so marvellous Ls fa,hhis and behaved in such per- fect discipline is a lasting credit to the children themse!ves, to their enthusiastic teachers, and in the highest degree to the Director of Education and his xcellent and devoted staff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>PRETTY SCENES AT PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PRETTY SCENES AT PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Pretty scenes were witnessed in the village of Penketh on the occasion of the Royal visit. Flags, 1 unting, and various loyal mottoes gave the district a f-stive appearance, and for some time before the approach of their Majestit s the roads were lined with eager crowds. Ihe scholars of the Council Etchool, in charge cf Mr. D. R. Griffiths, marched to a position in the centre of the village, and the parents of the children cre to be heartily congratulated upon the smart appearance of the youngsters. A very pleasing effect was created by the red, white and blue sashes which were worn. The pupils from the Friends' slloarding Schoal were also massed on the right of the Council Sch9ol, under Mr. Brown. . _</p>
        <p>• The momeni the Royal car was signalled loud cheers were raised, and as the King and Queen approached, the Penketh Tannery band struck up the National Anthem and the children and spectators joined fervently in the singing. The King was evidently touched by the welcome he received, and repeatedly bowed his acknowledgments, while the Queen bestowed many a gracious smile upon the people. The villagers had evidently resolved to make a great effort to convey their feelings of loyalty to their Majesties. Special comment may be made to the lettering across the road. The cardboard letters were prepared by the boys of the Council School,</p>
        <p>Continued in next column.</p>
        <p>PRETTY SCENES AT PENKETH.— Continued.</p>
        <p>and Mr. Mainley was responsible for the colouring, and Mr. Wo Award for the wiring across the road. As the Royal car proceeded at a very moderate speed, both old and young had an exceedingly good view of the King and Queen, and much satisfaction was expressed at the success of the proceedings. A special concession was made to the scholars of Great Sankey Council School, the Royal clr slowing at Penketh-lane Ends to give the children, who were massed in charge of Mr. George Clarke, a good opportunity of seeing their Majesties. In the evening the boys of the Council School visited their neighbours at the Friends' School, where a friendly cricket mach was indulged in. The Boarding School boys proved much too strong for their more youthful and less experienced opponents, but the game was greatly enjoyed by all, and the vanquithed will, no d )übt, take heart of grace, and learn valuable lessons from their experience of leather hunting.</p>
