      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Photograph by Burrell. SANKEY-STREET PAVEMENT RESERVED FOR CRIPPLES.</head>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell. SANKEY-STREET PAVEMENT RESERVED FOR CRIPPLES.</p>
        <p>King was about to cross the bridge leading to the works yard, he sinke a few kindly words to Peter Lythgoe Hill, who has seen 52 years' service with the firm. Mr. Hill was too deaf to hear what the King said, but, before patting with his Majesti, he made him a little set speech. "I am greatly honoured," he said, "to meet your Majesty. May your visit to Warrington carry with it pleasint recollections, and may God grant t) you both a long and prosperous</p>
        <p>reign." Emerging into the works yard their Majesties passed along a raised footway, the sides of which were open so that all the assembled workpeople could have a fall view. Boy Scouts formed an escort on one side of the platform. Tumultuous cheers rent the air and their Majesties repeatedly acknowledged the loyal demonstrations of this great cincourse. Before taking their departure, their Maj-?sties expressed their great delight with everything they had seen. They had been most interested in the various departments and were highly gratified at the splendid arrangements and the warmth of their reception.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>VETERANS.</head>
        <p>THE VETERANS.</p>
        <p>Near the RoyAl dais at th,3 Town Hall was a group which attracted more than pissing interest. It con- sited of aged military veterans who have fought in their country's service on famous battlefields, and whose medals testified to their true soldierliness.</p>
        <p>The following is alist of old soldiers who attended Colour-Sergeant Crane, of Winwick. Served in 45th Foot. Medals : Kaffir campign, 1852-3; Abyssinian campaign, 1868 ; Good conduct. Colour-Sergeant H. Corney, 6, Salisbury-street. Ssrved in 17th Leicester Regiment. Medals :Crimea, Turkish, long service, good conduct, meritorious service.</p>
        <p>Sergeant G. Cummings, 91, Wellfield-street, War- rington. Served in 88th Connaught Rangers, 2nd South Lancashire Regiment and 3rd South Lancashire Militia. Medals: Indian Mutiny with " Central India "clasp. Private J. Lyons, 106, Lythgoes-lane. Served in Gist Foot. Medals : Indiin Mutiny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>CHILDREN'S CORNER,</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S CORNER,</p>
        <p>. \ i''''''Xl.N, 'd.‘e.%/</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Conductd by UNCLE CALEB</head>
        <p>Conductd by UNCLE CALEB</p>
        <p>KING GEORGE'S CHORISTERS.</p>
        <p>When listening to the children who sang so sweetly in '• the Park last Monday it occurred to me to write a little about the King's choristers who sing in the private Chapel at St. James' Palace. There are ten boys in London who every Sunday and on .State occasions wear suits of clothes that in each instance cost something like £4O. The lads thus brilliantly attired are the ten choristers of the Royal Chapel, and when arrayed in their State garments they make a gorgeous picture.</p>
        <p>Scarlet cloth is the foundation of the costume. Bands of Royal purple between rows of heavy gold lace are the adornments. Old lace ruffles are worn at the neck and wrists. These ruffles are se valuable and so difficult to replace that they are only worn on the most important occasions. At other times white lawn bands take their place. The boys must take great care of their suits, which have to last three years at least. The "undress" suits provided for the boys are replaced every eight months. The choir is quite an historical institution, and many old customs as well as the old dress are retained from a distant past. During the last century it had among its members Sir Arthur Sullivan, Edward Lloyd, Sir John Goss and Dr. E. J. Hopkins. I had perhaps better mention one old custom that lingered in the choir. It is the right of the head boy to demand a guinea from any officer who enters wearing spurs. I suppose it was regarded as bad form for a soldier to enter a chapel with spurs upon his boots, and the head choir boy was allowed to place this fine upon anyone so doing.</p>
        <p>It is said that when Arthur Sullivan was the head boy, the Duke of Wellington would often walk in with spurs on his boots in order that he might have the pleasure of paying forfeit to his favourite chorister.</p>
        <p>WHEN THE KING WAS A MISCHIEVOUS BOY.</p>
        <p>As you have all been thinking about the King, I will tell you a story about one of his youthful pranks. On one occasion when he and his brother, the Duke of Clarence, were small boys, they were dining with Queen Victoria, their grandmother, at Windsor. They annoyed the old lady by being noisy at the table, and in anger she ordered them, as a mark of dis- grace, to get under the table and not to come out until she called for them.</p>
        <p>Sundry quiet chuckles came from under the clotll which suggested that some joke was being hatched, and in a few moments,her anger la.ing Cooled, the Queen announced that they might come out if they would promise to be good. As they emerged the Queen gave a look of astonishment and then broke into a fit of laughter. There they stood as naked as a pair of little savages. Every vestige of clothing had been removed.</p>
        <p>It had occurred to George that they might give grandma a shock, and this was his little plan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <p>THE UNSEEN CORD. There is an unseen cord which binds</p>
        <p>The whole wide world together; Through every human life it vrinds This one mysterious tether. It links all laces and all lands Throughout their spAn allotted; . . .</p>
        <p>And dealt' alone unties the strands Which God Himself has knotted.</p>
        <p>However humble be your lot, Flowe'er your hands are fettered, You cannot think a noble thought _ _ _</p>
        <p>But all the world is bettered. With every impulse, deed, or werd Wherein love blends with duty, A message speeds along the cord That gives the earth more beauty. Your unkind thought, your selfish deed, Is felt in farthest places ; Ther6 are no solitudes where greed And wrong can bide their faces. There are ao separate lives ; the chain Too subtle fir our seeing, Unites us all upon the plane Of universal being. _</p>
        <p>—Ella Wheeler Wilcox</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>AT WIDNES.</head>
        <p>AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>'What a Huge Crowd !"</p>
        <p>The Town Hall of Widnes never had such a glorious setting as on Monday, when the King and Queen spent ten minutes on a royal dais erected before it. The Town Hall square was profusely decked with garlands, figs, streamers, and floral devices. His Majesty expressed his regret to the Mayor that he could not visit one or two local industries owing to his programme being already so heavy. Their Majetties chatted with the Mayor before leaving, and the Queen said, "What a huge crowd you have." The Mayor said there were at least 10,000 present. The great occasion —the first visit of a monarch to Widnes —was celebrated by a fireworks display and a cycle parade in the evening.</p>
        <p>Continued on Page 10.</p>
      </div>
