      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>GOSSIP ABOUT LYMM.</head>
        <p>A GOSSIP ABOUT LYMM.</p>
        <p>Lym, Lime, or Lymm P—Tearing " Lymm from Warburton".— Legend of the Skeleton Hand.</p>
        <p>SOME EARLY INHABIT</p>
        <p>July is not the month for etymology, there- fore we won't worry as to whether it be Lym, Lyme, Lime, or Lymm. Possibly it is Lime (the Latin limes, or limit), and meant that it was the limit or last piece of Cheshire that was habitable before one stumbled into the marshy bogs that skirted the ancient Mersey, or possibly it meant lim or lime, an old name for mud.</p>
        <p>To-day it is not "the limit" of solid ground and mud pits don't spoil its beauty. On the other hand, it is a very good centre, and whatever of mud pits there may have been have gone to make some capital Cheshire soil.</p>
        <p>Of interesting things there are very many round the varied village of Lymm. The geologist starts us off by describing the place when monsters fifty feet long wandered over it.</p>
        <p>TRACES OF EARLY INHABITANTS. There is not the least doubt that the creatures were here, because they have left their footprints behind them, and when red stone is cut from the quarries workmen find huge and uncanny toe marks made upon muddy sand now hardened into stone. These "early inhabitants" left nothing in writing, and the date of their occupancy is a matter of enlightened guess work. A geolo- gist, whose book we were reading last week, suggests three million years as the space that lies between us and them. Geologists, of course, have methods of their own, and simple folks don't talk back to them. They all, as Professor Boyd Dawkins once said to the present writer, keep a colossal account in the Bank of Time, and don't fear to write big cheques. As we have mentioned these "first inhabitants," we had perhaps better say a little about them. They dug out a fairly perfect skeleton of one in Hungary not long ago. His skull weighed 3001b5., and the entire skeleton nearly a ton. The shape is that of a frog, with the scales of an alligator to cover the body.</p>
        <p>Scientists, wh-o have discoursed upon the creature, make the vivid but uncomfortable assertion that the throat and swallowing gear would take down a full-sized man at a gulp. Like many other relics of the dark ages, they can be studied most comfortably at a distance. We keep models of them in museums, admire them as much as circum- stances will permit, and attach to them the sweet and peaceful names of Labyrinthodon and Chierotherium.</p>
        <p>Even scientists don't profess to love the creatures or to wish them back. No doubt they were pretty enough in their frisky youth, but a full grown Labyrinthodon would have been a doubtful experiment as a beast of burden and a dismal failure as a pet, even though successfully trained and efficiently muzzled.</p>
        <p>It was-a wise arrangement that gave them their first innings upon the planet.</p>
        <p>YET AMERICA BEATS US.</p>
        <p>There would have been sufficient either in the creature or the name to make a whole county famous, were it not that America, with its irritating habit of showing the biggest of everything, had dug out from the Rocky Mountains, a bigger thing still. The Diplodocus, we are told, is at least 100 feet from the tip of its horny nose, along its giraffe-neck and toady body to the tip of an ugly tail. But Cheshire scores when it comes to brain measurement. The brain cavity of the American brute would not hold a good sized duck egg. Just think of it; a duck egg brain to manage a thirty-odd yard long body!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>SAXON DAYS.</head>
        <p>SAXON DAYS.</p>
        <p>It is a pretty big leap over the three million (geological) years to the days of the ruddy Saxon, yet it has to be taken before we get past the xons of unwritten history and reach the first bit that appears in black and white.</p>
        <p>In the time of the Conquest Lymm was held by Saxon owners, and their names float in, as the reader will have guessed already, in that most precious record known as the Domesday Book. William the Conqueror, with a country to give away, acted hand- somely towards his set of hungry followers. But he acted methodically, too, and within an incredibly short space a big book of vellum was bound together which contained a rough list and survey of the lands, owners and values of England. The district of Lymm is shown as being held before the Conquest by two Saxons, but filched away in the wreckage, and given to two Norman followers of Hugh Lupus. Here is the para- graph rendered into English:</p>
        <p>"The same Gilbert holds Lime. Ulviet held it and was free. There is one hide rate- able to the gelt. The land is two carucates. There are three bordars. There is half a church with half a virgate of land. There are woods half a league long and the same broad. In King Edward's time it was worth ten shillings, now twelve pence. It was found waste (by the Earl)." "The same Osberr holds Lime. Edward held it and was a free man. There is one hide rateable to the gelt. The land is four carucates. Edward holds it of him. He has one carucate, and there are also two neatherds and two villeins and four bordars, and half a church and a priest with half a virgate of land free. There is a wood half a league long and the same broad. In King Edward's time it was worth ten shillings, now eight shillings. It was found waste (by the Earl)."</p>
        <p>These entries, though giving only a fleet- ing glimpse of Lymm, are interesting as showing that the process which went on over the face of England obtained here also. The district is held by two Saxon freemen, Ulviet and Edward. They are dispossessed and the lands are given to Gilbert and Usbern, two followers of Hugh Lupus, Earl of Chester. The sombre word "waste " tells its own story of pillage, fire and worse. Osbern, it may be mentioned, was the Osbern Fitz Tezzon who held also Grappen- hall, Latchford and Appleton, and, who, as a good fighting man, was placed in charge of Doddleston Castle to keep back thefWelsh. The two Saxon lords placed the former owners under them as tenants and the new feudal order began.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>"HALF A CHURCH."</head>
        <p>"HALF A CHURCH."</p>
        <p>Before allowing the Domesday phrase 'half a church" to become a puzzle the reader must note that it occurs twice. This probably means that there was one church and that the new owners Gilbert and Osbern con- tributed to its support, each being respons- ible for half its maintenance.</p>
        <p>Out of this arrangement there appears to have grown a state of things which lasted for eight centuries, and which gave rise to a familiar Cheshire proverb. Upon the point we will quote Sir Peter Leycester, the best of old Cheshire anti- quaries, who wrote in 1666 : " Eiere hath been a church before the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>.NTS OF THE VILLAGE.</head>
        <p>.NTS OF THE VILLAGE.</p>
        <p>Norman Conquest, for it appears by Dooms- day Book that in the Conqueror's time Gilbert Venables, Baron of Kinderton, held half of this town, which Ulviet formerly held. This is half a church with half a virgate of land. Osbern, son of Tezzon, held the other half. He was ancestor of the Boydells, of Dodleston. One Edward held it of him, and he, too, had half a church, and a priest, with half a virgate of land, so that either lord had one half of the ,church; and so at this very day, i.e., in 1666; two par- son's are presented to Limme, who supply the ministry there, one one Sunday and the other the next Sunday after, and so by - course, Warburton of Arley, being now patron of one moiety of the said church, and Legh de West Hall, in' High Legh, patron of the other moiety, and one parson has one half of the tythes of Limme and the other parson hath the other half of the tythes. Gilbert de Limme released all his right in the advowson of the moiety of Limme church unto Thomas Legh de West Hall in High Legh, 1316; since which time that family have continued their right of pre- senting to that moiety which I conceive</p>
        <p>was the moiety belOnging to Gilbert Venables, in Doomsday Book." TEARING LYMM FROM WARBURTON.</p>
        <p>In simpler language this means that the dual patronage of the church, egtablished at the Conquest, was maintained. There were two patrons to one church, and each chose a parson. Many efforts were made tv) escape the complications without success, but it was finally accomplished in 1869, after giving eight centuries of annoyance. Out of the difficulty arose the proverb, "Like tearing Lymm from Warburton," which is still used in Cheshire to describe a difficult task. THE CHURCH.</p>
        <p>We know nothing of the church mentioned in the Domesday Book. Its style would be that of the small churches usually built in Saxon times, and it would probably be rebuilt by the Norman lords in the new style they brought from Normandy. Of neither the first nor second churches (assuming there were a second) have any parts survived. There are possible relics and we are told that deep below the floor of the present church workmen found a moulded base of red sandstone which had belodged to some earlier structure.</p>
        <p>A third church seems to have arisen early in the fourteenth century. Of this a portion remains in the form of an arch in the south wall. Two centuries later an appeal went out in the name of Sir Thomas Butler, Sir Piers Legh, and a string of other Cheshire worthies for funds towards a ,r" steeple of stone," then in building at " Limine Church, whereof Our Blessed Lady is the founder," without which the parish would. not be able to finish its work. The document states that there were three priests at Lyinm to pray for the benefactors.</p>
        <p>The fourteenth century church stood until 1850, and being considered insecure was replaced by the present building.. Surprisingly few relics adorn the church. Save for a tablet bearing the arms of Limme de Limme, Baron of Halton, there is little to indicate that we are in a church which has had the interest of illustrious families since the Conquest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>SKELETON HAND.</head>
        <p>THE SKELETON HAND.</p>
        <p>It must be confessed that the building looks modern, and, as viewed by the historian, it is scarcely interesting ; but lest it should be entirely colourless to the casual visitor we had better say that at least one tradition clings to its walls.</p>
        <p>A callous old dame, it is said, refused to walk to the village pump when -she wanted water, but went and held her bucket under the church spout. This was an age when the water from the church roof was sacred and cured nearly all the ills that flesh is heir to. But the hardened crony cared nothing for cries of sacrilege, until one day when holding the bucket a skeleton hand and arm shot forth from the spout struck her a blow and seizing the pail handle drew it swiftly up the spout</p>
        <p>Presumably it has been "up the spput : " ever since, for the dirge concludes : Ne'er was the pail nor hand I ween, By mortal eye again e'er seen. This is a very interesting yarn to those who can read things with the historical eye. It is a relic of the days when village folks were taught that everything that belonged to the church was sacred even to the water that had trickled down a consecrated gutter, and that everybody who robbed the church came to grief. A.M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>AMBULANCE WORK.</head>
        <p>AMBULANCE WORK.</p>
        <p>3,000 Drinks in " Examiner " Cups.</p>
        <p>Fortunately Monday was not excessively hot, and there were no accidents of a seri( us nature. At the Town Hall a photographer had a fit, and was attended by the Mayor just before the arrival of the King. Excellent ambulance arrangements had been made for coping with the crowded streets by Corps Superintendent Waring, Quartermaster H. I'. Williams (Voluntary Aid Detachment), and Divisional Secretary A. E. Spann. Forty-nine officers and men and nursing sisters, under the charge of Superintendent Baldry, were stationed along the line of the Royal route, and attended to 143 cases of fainting, while nearly 3,000 persons were supplied with water in sfecial sanitary paper cups presented to the Warringto3 Corps by the proprietors of the " Warrington Examiner."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>FIRE BRIGADE CONTESTS.</head>
        <p>FIRE BRIGADE CONTESTS.</p>
        <p>Local Brigades Take Part.</p>
        <p>On Saturday the annual competitions in connection with the North-Westera district of the National Fire Brigade Union took place at Helsby, n large crowd of people attending. His Majesty the King is the patron of the Union, and the president of the district is the Earl of Haddingtou. 'I here were eighty-one" entries for the different competitions, and grest keenness was shown by the different 'brigades. During the afternoon Captain the Hon. H. R. Arden-Baillie-Hamilton and Lady Cicely Arden-Baillie-Hamilton were present, and at the conclusion of the competitions the latter pre- sented the prizes, in the unavoidable absence of the Earl of Haddington. The competitions resulted as follows :</p>
        <p>Four-Men Hydrant Drill for the President's Cup (presented by Lord Haddington) and medal for the chief officer of the brigade —l, Alpraham, 19 4-5 sec. ; 2, Know Mills, 20 2-5 sec. ; 3, Wyre Dock (holders), 21 1-5 sec. ; 4, Greenall, Whitley and Co., 23 2-5 s-c. Four-Men Manual Drill (dry) for the Nantwich Challenge Cup.-1, Macclesfield Volunteer, 19 4-5 sec. ; 2, Alpraham holders), 211.5 sec. ;3, Hoole, 21 2-5 sec. Steamer Wet Drill for the Crosfield Steamer Challenge Shield.-1, Hightield Tannery, 49 3-5 sec.; 2, Castner- Kellner, 50 sec. ; 3, Crosfleld's (holders), 51 3-5 sec. Manual Wet Drill for the Tarporley Challenge Cup.- 1, Tarporley, 21 1-5 sec. ; 2, Alpraham, 25 sec.; 3, Iloole, 29 sec._</p>
        <p>_ The, Hose Cart Challenge Shield (presented by Surgeon-Major Harrison, Chester).-1, Helsby, 30 Sec.; 2, Alpraham, 30 1-5 sec. ; 3, Greenell, Whitley and Co , 31 sec.</p>
        <p>Girls' Drill.-1, Elelsby (No. 2), 18 2-5 sec. ; 2, llelslu (No. 1), 19 sec.; 3, Winwivlc Asylum, 32 2-5 sec.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, July 12, 1913.</p>
      </div>
