        <p>ing the workmen manufacture sheets of plate glass. From the unlovely streets of ct. Helens, the King and Queen passed to the brightness and prosperity of Southport, where the authorities had refrained from The second day of the Royal visit to Lancashire I over-decoration, and had allowed the chief glory of enabled the King and Queen to see some sharply the town—the tree-bordered length of Lord-street contrasted aspects of the county's life. They motored to look as beautiful as it is. from Knowsley in the morning to t. Helens, where, The stay at Southport was brief' but at Preston,the 8 • next stopping-place, a considerable time was spent, after the usual civic reception, they spent the best-part for here luncheon was taken and a visit paid to the of an hour in the great Pilkington glassworks watch- mill of Messrs. Horrockscs, Crewdson, and Co.</p>
