      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>OLD WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>OLD WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>the Mayoral procession came down Bridge-street, The Mayor accompanied by Aldermen, Town Coun- cillors and other prominent townsmen, assembled outside the bridge. After a few seconds, in which to allow the cheering to subside, the Mayor stepped to the bridge entrance and said "This bridge has been formally opened by his Majesty King George V. for the use of the inhabitants of Warrington and the public generally; God save the King," Continuing, his Worship said ; "I have great pleasure in asking Alderman Smethurst (chairman of the Bridges Committee) to unveil this tablet. I will only say a few words. This is not a time for speech-making, and there is some difficulty on the part of the police in keeping the people—who are full of enthusiasm—back. Mr. Alderman Smethurst has taken enormous interest in this under- taking. Some might have thought that the Mayor ought to have performed this ceremony, but I considered it more appropriate on the grounds I have named that Alderman Smethurst should do so." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>Photograph by Billies, Warrington.</head>
        <p>Photograph by Billies, Warrington.</p>
        <p>and trust that the latter partion will be carried or t as successfully as this has been. Under the guidance of Mr. Webster and his associates, the bridge will b 3 carried to a successful issue, and, we sincerely hope, without any accident whatever. I am told by Mt Webster that it is hoped to have this bridge finishei in about nine months from now. I have promised him a new hat if he achieves that, but he does not want. one, sal shall get out of that bargain, even if it is finished in time." (Laughter And applalse.) On the invitation of the Mayor, the Deputy Mayor (Alderman Forshaw), A'derwan Smethurst and Mr. Webster then accompanied his Worship over the bridge. The rest of the civic pry aickly followed, and then passing over the old bridge t hey returned in processional order to the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>The tablet, which is fixed in a paia. oa the pedestal of the bridge, is inscribed :</p>
        <p>"This Bridge was opened by his Majesty KING GEORGE V. July 7th, 1913. Councillor G. W. JOSEPH, M.D., J.P., Mayor. Alderman JAMES SMETHURST, J.P,, Chairman of the Bridges Committee. J. WEBSTER, M.lnst. C.E. M.I. Mech.E . Engineer, "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>DISAPPEARING FRIEND _ And old buildings that have already been cleared away to widen Knutsford-road and give a footing to the new struclare.</head>
        <p>A DISAPPEARING FRIEND _ And old buildings that have already been cleared away to widen Knutsford-road and give a footing to the new struclare.</p>
        <p>"I REMEMBER' REMINIS(JENCES AND STORIES OF OLD WARRINGTON. BY</p>
        <p>V ARIOUS CONTRIBUTORS.</p>
        <p>COCAC-FIGHTING IN ARPLEY MEADOWS.</p>
        <p>11. lit ii etty, Gentlemanly Sport the,n, Times be different now.</p>
        <p>r I As lam only in my sixty-fl' it cannot go back very far in rriern°ll.2 li-dog am old enough to remember cock-lig-nsed in Arpley Meadows and the men whc)nn-t to arrange the fights. The subject iS ',4010 pleasant on, yet I suppose it has a .ce; coo interest. I will not linger over the Cr of it, but only say what I remember ha'" seen. wad, The birds were game cocks of a sPeeial el and were bred and trained for fighting. tho were fed upon food calculated to niaVv t fierce, the chief thing being raw bee' ;;;stal into small pieces. Upon their claws '; spurs were fastened. I have seen silvorts.rteel. but the ones commonly used were o' ['he cocks were brought in bags' tt., the were placed opposite to each other,:-ntber ring and then proceeded to tear each -thehl to pieces. They fought until one el, :ilisre was killed, and the one that remaille' was the victor. d,hole Of course, gambling lay behind the "h•rds business. It went on freely, as the,,i/oro fought, and the excitement of the ePee'</p>
        <p>often became a frenzy. - lace Ail)ley Meadows was a favourite Piiag, both for prize fighting and for cock-figh;ie. and Sunday morning was the favourite tbleoic For a long period there was little or 11° °force upon the contests, but when the Pollicem rise became effective, the cock-fighters had t00 some caution. The very last fight 'it LW saw was broken up almost as soon as begun. Mr. Hunt, a late Chief Conswoot had just come to the town, and rnst known to many people. The cry w„e'ti ' that the new chief was approaching, crowd scattered across the meadoW'i tile was not Mr. Hunt, but the mention ° name caused the break-up of the ring. Coc7c- Fighting Superstitions Practices. if I Perhaps it will interest your reauertadf copy a passage from a book written hY •5 an Milnes Gaskell on "Shropshire." There tni account of a talk with an old Shronshire„per; in which he tells of some cock-fighters' s-toln stitions. I have heard the same thingsarere, by my father of Warrington bird re'n but this account puts it better than I c3re bis "They war merry times." the° voice sank almost to a whisper. " , there Was cock-fightin.' " yov "Cock-fighting," I inquired. "ffave ever seen much of that ?" Theoi " Lor, love yer !" retorted Master rse balds, with kindly contempt. "Of 001-1-011 have, and a prettier, more gentil sport I h'aint acquainted with. I itulto of the good old time when every sciti," the, his own main of cocks, and manY we' farmers as had a good clutch, and gresd 'tin' the pride of the missus in rearin' a gc° ore, Folks 'used to say at Ludlow as thers,4l some as thought more of their cocks ft',-itr their own souls. Why, marm, when Lti little 'un, we should have thought a tin got poor benighted one-horse place as haall,i old its coitk-pit. There used," continuent i 4 Timothy, "to be a fine place beyond sq`'`Oi ,now the vicarage, where they used. t the em regularly. on Easter Monday, and a't May Fair at Wenlock. Every serval.' ed to as had a touch of sport in his b