      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>AT THE BRIDGE,</head>
        <p>AT THE BRIDGE,</p>
        <p>Their Majesties having passed up Bridge-street, the crowd at the Mersey end of the town concentrated o n the bridge. All were eager to witness the severing of the ribbon in token of the fact that the King had pressed the button at the Town Hall and opened the first section of the magnificent structure, which is to connect two counties. Across the northern end of the bridge was a red, white and blue ribbon, with a central pillar draped in red and surmounted with pink carnations. All eyes were fixed upon the ribbon, for it was known that when his Majesty touched the electric button at the Town Hall, the pretty symbol would fall on either side of the The approach to the bridge was guarded by the Territorials and Army Service Corps, and during the period of waiting for the Mayoral procession the band of the Territorials played admirable selections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>'IN THE NAME OF THE KING."</head>
        <p>'IN THE NAME OF THE KING."</p>
        <p>At 3.16 there was a loud report, and a rocket soared high over Arpley. Simultmeously the ribbon was severed, and it was known to all that the King had opened the bridge. The cheering was renewed who n</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-07-12_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>TO BE COMPLETED IN NINE MONTHS</head>
        <p>TO BE COMPLETED IN NINE MONTHS</p>
        <p>In unveiling the tablet by pulling -a silk en cord, Alderman Smethurst said : "It gives me great pleasure to unveil this tablet in commemoration of the completion of the first part of this bridge. I hope</p>
        <p>From Preston the rcute lay thrcugh the Fy ldc to Lytham, St. Annes, and Blackpool. Although only a short stay was poslible at Blackpool, the King a. d Queen saw Lancashire's famous holiday resent at its very best. Sea, sun, and breeze all helped to render their .progress along the magnificent promanad 3-- lavishly decorated at.d crowded from end to end - a splendid spectacle, and it is not surpriska to hear the King and Queen both expressed the plezzure their brief visit bad given them.</p>
      </div>
