        <p>thanks were accorded to Mr. Ben Bradfnrd, the friends who kindly lent conveyances, the Latchford Bugle Band &amp;c. Before starting the homeward journey, the com- pany gathered around the house, and sang "God be with you till we meet again." ORFORD• CIIURCH WORKERS' EXCURSION.—The choir, Sunday school teachers and olher Church workers, along with a few friends, had their annual excursion on Saturday to New Brighton. Between and 60 made the jouraey, the majority going by the 8.50 a.m. from the Central Station. On arrival at Liver- pool, the party broke up, some visiting the Exhibition, and others crossing the river to have a full dav at New Brighton. They all met again at five o'clock for tea in the Tower Grounds. A most enjoyable day was spett, the weather proving kind, and the party returned home by the 930 express. The whole of the arrange- ments were in the hands of Nlr. W. Povey. WIN WICK. ANNU AL SPORTS. —The annual asylum sports took place on Thursday in good weather. There were races for each division of tte patients and the attendants, and such extra events as crltch the bellman for the nurses, and greasy pole climbing for a ham, by the married attendants. The male attendants' bicycle race resulted :—l, R. Deneh, C. Evans, J. Jones. Nurses' bicycle race :I, B. Ards. Mrs. Simpson, the superin- tendent's wife, presented the prizes. RISLEY. INDEPENDENT METHODIST Cu RCH, FEARNHEA D.— An " open" meeting was observed in the school on Sunday afternoon, a large cmgregation being present. Numerous songs and recitations were given by both young and old scholars. Mr. C. H. Harrison delivered a short address. Mr. T. Cook occupied the chair. Miss G. Hughes presied at the piano. PENK ETH• SCHOOL TRIP.—The teachers and emir of the Wesleyan Church and Sunday school had their annual cuting on Saturday, the place selected being Winder- me: e. A pleasant time was spent in visiting Ambleside and its beautiful surroundings. Mr. G. Carman had made excellent arrangements. FANCY DRESS. The reference to the " stocks of clothing," in the report which has awakened reminiscences, sends me back in thought to an occasion when, on my way to an inquest at that same country workhouse, I was overtaken by a tremendous thunderstorm, and, the situation being exposed and shelter- less, I was speedily soaked to the skin. In this plight I presented myself to the matron and begged for the loan of pauper garments from her store. Somehow she hardly liked the idea, and called to my aid the porter, who lent me his cycling suit. Into these habiliments r changed with alacrity,while my wet things were taken to the drying house. After all, it was rather regrettable that I was not allowed to present myself before the Coroner and jury in the garb of an inmate. I fancy the former, an irascible old " party," who prided himself on standing no nonsense, would have been scandalised.- Old Journalist.</p>
