        <p>at the same time he can compulsory bring about a meeting of both parties in his presence, and if his utmost efforts are unsuccessful in leading them to a unanimous agreement, he Gin report the case to the Minister of Labour, whe, in his turn, and without a minute's delay, can refer the mstter for jud-oial adjust- ment to the Arbitration Curt. "It is thus possible," says the Bon. G. S. B eSy, the przsent Minister of Labour in New South Wales, whose experience of this kind of legislation has b )en un'Tse a.d import-Int, "for the Arbitration Court to be engaged in actual inquiry within a few dsys after the report of the threatened trouble to the Commissioners."—J. H. Harley, in the " Contempt's. ry Review " for July."</p>
