        <p>CRICKET. A BRILLIANT INNINGS. George Woodcock Scores 81 for Warrington Against Cheetham. When Cheetham visited Warrington a few weeks ago, the home eleven were beaten almost on time, after an exciting finish. Saturday saw the return engagement, and the outcome was a draw, Warrington being 31 runs behind, with three wickets to fall. The outstanding feature of the match was a brilliant display by George Woodcock, who carried out his bat with 81 to his credit. He ferced the pace, hut his cricket was without a blemish. He scored 22 in one over, and also hit a grand six—a great feat at Cheetham, considering the exceptionally large dimensions of the field. He also did some useful bowling and captured two wickets for (4.0 runs. FIRST TEAM AVERAGES. BATTING. Not Inns. Out. Out. Runs. A'ge G. Woodcock S. L. Partridge 7 6 1 207 34'0 W. Richardson B. Silcock J. Bradburn T. Wright J. Callon 8 8 0 107 133 Hannam S. Baxter Rev. H. Gray 3 3 0 8 26 J. W. Pearson A. Duckworth W. Bennett BOWLING ANALYSES. 0. 51. R. W. A'ge. Rev. IT. Gray ........ 47 21 73 15 438 S. L. P.rtridge Hannam l5 t 8 321 24 13•3 G. Wocdc,ck W. Bennett B. Silcock Warrington 2nd lost by 18 runs to Cheetham 2nd. At one stage the outlook for the visitca-s was anything but promising, for 8 wickets were down with less than 50 rune on the score sheet. Scholes and Jackson' how- •ver, made a great stand, and carried the innings to 130 before they were separated. They took liberties with the bowling, but fortune favoured them and their partnership came at a most opportune time for Cheatham. Warrington reap( nc"el with 120, several of the batsmen being rather unlucky. J. Thompson failed to judge s short run and was run out at 17, Bert Lowe was stumped oft the wicket keeper's pads when he had made 29, whilst W. 11. Bell and W. Hinds played on. Broome bowled exceedingly well and captured eight wickets for 45 runs. Altogether it was a sporting and enjoyable match. Owing to the holidays there was no play in the War- rington and District League, the match oetween Cairo- street and "Divex" being postponed until August 23rd. -- Bold-street Wesleyans accounted for Penketh Friends School by a margin of 11 runs. W. Stringer captured six wickets for 12 runs, and made nine for his side, whilst R. Weaver got three a ickets at a cost of 7 runs. Friendly Matches. CHEETHAM v WARRINGTON. At Cheetham on Saturday. Scores : Cheetham. Warrington. II J Milsom c Duck- WRichardsen bSinclair 29 worth b Partridge ... 55 J Callon c Boumphrey H Buckley run out H W Boumphrey b T Wright c Buckley b Partridge ll Holt C N Holt b Bennett A H Milsom b Bennett 6 clair F Foote b Partridge 25 J Bradburn b Sinclair 2 W E Ainley c Baxter b G Woadcock not out... 81 Woodcock 8 S Baxter c Bonn- phrey H Blight c Bradburn b b Sinclair 1 Bennett J Yates c Boumphrey F Stubbs b Partridge ... 21 b Sinclair. C H Sharp not out T H Sinclair c Partridge b Woodcock 2 Extras 8 Extras Total 905 Total (for 7) 174 BOWLING ANALYSIS. Cheetham. Warring ton. 0. at. R. W. O. M. B. W. Hannam W Benrett l4 1 62 3 H 81ight...... 16 4 33 0 S L Partridge 16 2 50 4 T II Sinclair 15 2 46 5 G Woodecck... 6 0 26 2 H Boumphrey 1 0 22 0 F Foote ...... 3 025 0</p>
